 Thank you.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:59.980
 Thank you.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:29.980
 Thank you.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.540
 All right.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.220
 Welcome to our budget work session.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.940
 It is Tuesday, April 28th at 3.02.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.000
 And we'll go ahead and call this meeting to order.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:48.000
 The first item of business today, we're going to turn the time right over to Vince, our engineer,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.580
 to discuss some water and other items that he knows.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:01:59.660
 Sounds good.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.860
 Let me get this PowerPoint started here.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:14.400
 There we go.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:22.380
 There we go.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:34.920
 So I have three different items to present to you today.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.320
 Water, wastewater, and our stormwater projects.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.500
 These are all capital projects that we're proposing to fund for next year.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.900
 First of all, water.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.760
 These projects on this slide are all projects that we're currently working on.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.180
 They're projects that are in different phases of actual completion.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:04.220
 I'll go through each one individually, but these are items that we have budgeted in past years.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.320
 We're going to roll that budget into the current fiscal year.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:16.060
 And then what you're seeing here are additional funds that we're requesting for this year to complete these projects.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:22.580
 Our 4 million gallon tank, that's the one up at Scarlet Ridge.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:23.880
 It's currently under construction.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.640
 The floor is completely poured.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.400
 A few of the columns are poured.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.360
 We have two wall sections that are poured.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.180
 And so they're pouring wall sections every one or two weeks.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.580
 And things are moving ahead well.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.840
 Things are looking good on that project.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:47.060
 I think overall project budget was around $2.5 million to complete it this year.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.100
 We're asking for the additional $700,000 just to wrap up construction.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.620
 Any questions on that one?

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.280
 So $3.2 is the total overall cost for the construction of that tank?

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.040
 Yep, approximately.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.520
 That's better than the last one we did, I think.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.820
 Upgrade well 5.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:16.800
 What we've done on well 5 to date, I guess first of all, well 5 is up hidden hollow.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.420
 Right behind the elementary school.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:24.780
 That pumps water up to our two different tanks that are up hidden hollow canyon up there.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:33.460
 Well 5 specifically, it has capacity to pump more water than we have a pump for at this time.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.160
 And so the upgrade on well 5 was upgrading pumps.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.900
 But in order to upgrade those pumps, of course, we have to upgrade all the electrical systems that go along with it.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.260
 And so we've installed a backup generator on that also.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:53.100
 All of those items are really long lead type of items.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.580
 And so we ordered those probably a year and a half ago.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:58.540
 Some of the items have arrived.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:04:59.840
 Some are still out there.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.840
 It should be getting close.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:09.680
 What we're budgeting for this year is to hire a contractor, get everything installed and ready to go.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:10.660
 Functional.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.360
 Benz, can you speak to the additional capacity that's going to give us?

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.200
 What are we hoping to gain from that?

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.780
 So I think right now, Mac would know better.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:24.760
 He's not here today.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.220
 But I think right now we're at 2,000 to 2,500 gallons per minute.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.240
 We're hoping to bump it up to 3,000, 3,500 gallons per minute.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.500
 So adding an extra 1,000 gallons per minute.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.920
 And then well 7 on Unity Pass.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:50.560
 That's the well that we've been drilling on for a year and a half or so.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:55.620
 Right off of Pony Express, right next to the middle school.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:56.320
 Or not the middle school.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.260
 The elementary school that's right there at Hidden Hollow.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.480
 Obviously, we've drilled it.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.580
 We have an engineer hired at Bowen and Collins.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.420
 They're doing the design on that.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:14.040
 That was all in the budget that we've already received in the past year.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:22.060
 For this next year, the plan is to build the well house, install the pumps, install all the electrical equipment.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:26.320
 And so this would be budgeting to complete that project with everything.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:32.120
 And so hopefully by the end of 2027, we would have another functioning well.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:35.620
 I'm going to bug you again.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.080
 What's the total overall cost then for that well?

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:40.520
 The overall cost for that well?

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.780
 I'm trying to remember what the drilling costs were.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.040
 I think they were close to $2 million for the drilling.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:51.300
 And so $4, $4.5 million.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:53.840
 Didn't we have some change orders along the way too?

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:55.280
 Maybe Kimberly can speak to that.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:01.800
 Because I know that well has been in process for quite a number of years with bits that still live at the bottom and stuff.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:09.520
 So I think as we discussed with the driller, because he didn't make his actual goal.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.000
 We were trying to get down to 1,500 feet as far as depth.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:14.540
 He never got there.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:19.120
 He had issues with drilling heads, losing drilling heads down the hole and things like that.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:25.120
 And it was at a point where we were worried about losing the well completely and having to start all over.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:34.500
 And so because he didn't make all the way down to his full depth, we did negotiate with him.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:37.440
 And I think we came in under budget on that.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:42.040
 We didn't get the full depth, but we also didn't pay him everything that was in the original contract.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:49.640
 Well, I think that's what they need to know here is that it was a per foot bid, right, and he didn't get there.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.900
 So what was the amount maybe that we paid?

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.060
 He was obviously a third short.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:57.400
 Did we short him a third money?

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.220
 I know there was some change orders, so I don't...

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:08.280
 Yeah, and it doesn't come out exactly even because there's mobilization costs and stuff that would have been the same no matter how deep he went.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:12.460
 So do we lose capacity then because he didn't get further down?

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:20.120
 So when we did our pump test, after he had finished drilling, we were able to get over 3,000 gallons per minute.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:23.600
 And so it looks like it's a good well.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:35.080
 As far as production, the difference is if we would have gone all the way down to the 1,500, as our aquifer goes up and down, it will vary a lot from year to year.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.640
 And if we get into a drought cycle of multiple years, it will drop.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:43.800
 And there is a chance that we could drop it below where we drilled.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.020
 And so that's...

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.160
 Is there a way to remedy that once we build the well house or...

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.680
 I mean, you take the roof off and keep drilling?

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:51.200
 Right.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:52.680
 There's really not.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:53.720
 No.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:54.920
 So we're at 1,000 feet.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:56.000
 We're at 1,000 feet.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.580
 Yeah, we can't go back and increase it.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.800
 Do we want to stop there?

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:06.420
 Or is there an opportunity for us to get to the mark that we need to get to?

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.320
 Or is this just, it is what it is now?

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:10.840
 For this well, it is what it is.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:17.420
 I think what we'll do instead of that is make sure we have other wells in other places so we have redundant sources.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:22.600
 And so if we get to that point where we do draw our aquifer down that far, we have other sources.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.500
 So it'll be the first one that comes offline, basically.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:25.020
 Yeah.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:25.760
 Yeah.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:26.320
 Exactly.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:37.780
 Which brings us to well 9, which is basically a redundant well for well 7.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:41.920
 I mean, we do need the capacity because of the growth, but it is a redundant well also.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:48.580
 This one is at the end of Lone Tree, actually closer to the airport on the bottom end.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:54.980
 And this one, we have funding in the past funding cycle to start drilling on that.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.500
 And we're going to start, we've already hired a driller.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:03.420
 He's going to start in July just with timeframes and other things he had going on.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.100
 He can't start until July, but we'll get him drilling in July.

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:12.560
 And so the funding that we had from last year for that drilling will move to the next year.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.160
 And then we'll just move on to the next steps of that one.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:25.700
 For this funding cycle, this $3.5 million will be full engineering design, and then well house pumps, all the electrical equipment.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:36.240
 This one also has a length of pipe, whereas the well 7, it was right next to Pony Express where we have pipelines that could tie right in.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:45.100
 And this one, we have to go a little ways with our pipelines, so you'll see it's a little more expensive than the other well, but it's because we're adding on a pipeline section to it also.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:52.960
 For what was in the current budget, Vince, for me, I'm just trying to, I recognize that each well is individual and different.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:54.140
 I mean, I understand that.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:11:00.140
 But I'm trying to just kind of draw some conclusions that we're about $4.5 million, $5 million to put in a well.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.400
 It's going to fluctuate a million, a million and a half, depending upon the issues that we have.

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:09.440
 And our hope is that we're going to get 3,000 gallons per minute.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:10.600
 That's a good well.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:12.920
 I know that some of them don't produce that.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.500
 Drilling wells is a bit of a gamble.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:18.140
 Sure.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:21.760
 You know, that's why you hire somebody to water wage and tell you where to drill a hole.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.420
 So I'm just in my head.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:26.760
 I'm trying to wrap my head around all that.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:28.240
 So what did we have previously in our budget?

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:28.940
 A million bucks?

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:30.820
 Is that what we already budgeted for this, or do you know?

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:31.440
 For the drilling?

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:32.500
 I don't remember.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:34.580
 I don't know, Kimberly, if you have that number.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:40.420
 I believe originally, so when we do our wells, we typically will break it out into a couple

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:44.300
 of different, so you have the drilling component, and then we'll have the pump house and connecting

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:45.260
 the line to it.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:53.020
 So I do at least believe for well number nine, that the original was probably in the 3 million

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:55.160
 to 3 and a half million, almost that number.

00:11:55.700 --> 00:12:04.660
 When we did Unity Pass, that project, just in drilling the well, not even the pump house

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:06.440
 itself, was over a $5 million.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:12.660
 Typically, the drilling part for us, when we're looking at estimates, is about $3.5 million,

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.460
 and then historically, it's been another $2.5 million, $3 million to do the pump house and

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:18.120
 connect the line.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:22.920
 But I do know there's a lot, again, it depends on the train, everything that's involved with

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:23.160
 it.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.860
 That's just helpful information to be able to share with the general public, that we're sometimes

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.660
 $5 to $7 million to put in a well.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:29.380
 Yeah.

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:30.680
 Thanks.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:38.360
 Real quick, are there any adjustments that need to be made to what we're asking for impact

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:42.060
 fee-wise, if it's more than what we anticipate originally?

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:44.000
 Yes.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:49.460
 And the process to that would be we would have to update our IFFP and get that approved, and

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:53.080
 then go to the IFA and get that approved also.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:57.900
 And so there is a process to do that, and we're planning on more frequent updates than

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:01.360
 we have done in the past to account for some of these rising costs.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:05.500
 As we know, there's been a lot of inflation in the last even couple of years that make

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:06.740
 a big difference in our costs.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:14.060
 Do you know the last time, Steph, we did look at what our impact fees are for water specifically?

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:22.860
 So I don't know the last time the IFA was approved officially, but we have one right now that's

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:27.680
 under consideration that has all these numbers in it, and so you should be seeing that.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.340
 We'll bring it to council in a month or two.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:30.920
 Thank you.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:31.460
 Yep.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:38.120
 So these projects are all new projects.

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:45.940
 As you can see, the top three are different sections of our SR-73 project.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:50.620
 And the reason I broke them out is because that's the way they were broken out in our master

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:51.020
 plan.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:56.740
 Now, at this point, we're talking about trying to do these all at the same time so that

00:13:56.740 --> 00:14:03.640
 we get one continuous project, cheaper mobilization and things like that is just more efficient

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:05.640
 to do them all at the same time if that's possible.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:10.540
 Can you just give us kind of a general idea of what work is being done in that project?

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:11.040
 Yep.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:11.040
 Yep.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:11.540
 Yep.

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:12.040
 Yep.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:17.440
 In fact, I have a slide that kind of shows the area.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:22.340
 So this is Saratoga Springs over here.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:24.100
 This is SR-73.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:32.160
 So right up above SR-73 in Saratoga Springs, we have what we call the North Terminal Reservoir.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:33.680
 That's Central Utah's main reservoir.

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:37.220
 They built it several years ago for this purpose.

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:41.320
 Not just for Eagle Mountain specifically, but they pump water up to Jordan Valley Water Conservancy

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:46.580
 District and I know Saratoga and Lehigh all have water there too.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:52.540
 But the plan is to connect to that reservoir and then bring this pipeline down all the way

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:56.080
 to Airport Road.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:15:00.780
 And like I said, it was originally cut into three sections and they weren't equal sections

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:05.020
 as you can kind of see by the prices, but in the master plan, it was cut into three different

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:06.020
 sections.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:08.320
 But again, we're planning to do everything at once.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:13.280
 Is the intent to do that before they start construction on SR-73?

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:14.780
 Is that the plan?

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:15.780
 Yeah.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:16.780
 Yeah.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:20.080
 It would be nice to kind of be in and out and out of their way.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:21.080
 Yep.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:34.680
 So, Waterline from Sage Valley to Mountain, Mid Valley Road is what that is.

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:40.380
 That's actually paralleling our Airport Road that we just put in and it connects onto the

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:46.240
 end of the SR-73 pipeline and just takes that south into the city to where more of the development

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:50.840
 is so we can get that central Utah down into kind of the heart of the city.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:54.840
 Will that pipe, where will that be placed?

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:57.840
 Is that, I know the sewer line kind of runs right underneath the road.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:58.840
 Right.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:00.840
 So when you put the water line in, does that go east-west?

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:01.840
 What is that easement?

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:02.840
 I'm just curious.

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:08.400
 So, we will look at that when we do design.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:12.480
 It will depend on how much right-of-way we have on each side.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.360
 And so yeah, that's not determined yet.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:17.840
 That will be determined as part of the design process.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:23.540
 And we try to keep it flexible because there's other utilities around that too.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:24.920
 The airport is right there too.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:29.420
 And that will be a factor that we look at as far as access in and out of there and things

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:30.420
 like that.

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:33.260
 And so we will look at all of that when we do the full design on it.

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:34.260
 Okay.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:37.600
 And then maybe just a general question for me as a new council member.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:44.780
 Funding from these different water projects, are they coming from a specific fee that's

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.780
 being imposed somewhere for some development?

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:49.160
 Or are they coming from it?

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:50.160
 Yeah.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:51.160
 Yeah.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.080
 The funding for this is all through impact fees.

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:54.080
 Okay.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:55.080
 It's due to the growth of the city.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:58.940
 And so every house gets an assessment for that impact fee.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:04.420
 So that $4 million, for example, in this project is being allocated back to the developer.

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:05.420
 It is.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:06.420
 It is.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:16.360
 On this one, SR73, I guess not the airport pipeline that we're talking about now, but SR73,

00:17:16.360 --> 00:17:21.900
 we're talking about doing that as part of the bond that we've done also.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.720
 And then that bond will be paid back with impact fees.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:25.720
 Okay.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:26.720
 Yep.

