 area eventually.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:47.980
 Oh, good.

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:48.980
 Okay.

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:49.980
 So as development happens along the airport, right?

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:50.980
 I just want to make sure we're considering.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:51.980
 Yeah.

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:52.980
 All the growth.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:53.980
 Yeah.

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:54.980
 Yeah.

00:17:54.980 --> 00:18:00.960
 Now, as it is right now, that 16-inch actually ties into an 8-inch line, which you never like

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:04.240
 to downsize a sewer line like that from a larger to smaller.

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:05.240
 Where is that?

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:11.940
 That happens right at the south end of Airport Road, and it ties into the Overland subdivision.

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:13.540
 And that's a temporary connection.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:14.540
 Okay.

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:18.460
 Because one of our other projects we'll talk about today will take that line and continue

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:19.460
 it.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:20.460
 Okay.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:21.460
 Thanks.

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:29.840
 Just to -- for me understanding, when we run the line up Eagle Mountain Boulevard, it will

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:30.840
 be a separate line.

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:35.460
 We're not going to be running from that 16-inch line across to the west, is that correct?

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:36.460
 For the sewer?

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:37.460
 Yeah.

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:38.460
 Yeah.

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:42.740
 There's a west trunk line that's a little further out in our planning that will pick up everything

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:44.460
 on the west side of the city.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:45.460
 Okay.

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:46.460
 Yep.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:47.460
 Thanks.

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:48.460
 Yep.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:49.460
 Okay.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:54.840
 The last one, pipeline from Pony Express Parkway to Airport Road.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:59.840
 This one is basically a second phase of the one we just talked about.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:05.220
 It's going to connect on there, and this is water line, not sewer line again.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:09.480
 But we're going to connect on to the water line that we're going to run parallel to Airport

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:12.600
 Road and just take it all the way to Pony Express.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:15.600
 And that will give us a good loop in our water system.

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:21.940
 In water systems, we love looping, we love redundancy, just so we can shuffle the water where we

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:22.940
 need it.

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:29.520
 But does this loop that we're going to add, I mean, with the SR 73 and all of that coming

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:33.700
 back down to Pony Express Parkway, does that create additional capacity?

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:38.780
 I'm assuming it does, to be able to take delivery of more of that acre feet that we have, our contract

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:39.780
 with Central Utah Water.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:40.780
 It does, for sure.

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:41.780
 Yep.

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:45.560
 Will it allow us to take the full capacity of that contract, or is it just a portion?

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:46.560
 Yes.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:50.560
 The plan is to design this so that we can get the full capacity of our Central Utah.

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:51.560
 Thank you.

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:52.560
 Yeah.

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:58.200
 It will probably be oversized for now, but in the long term we won't have to replace it.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:19:59.200
 Yeah.

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:10.540
 So, yeah, there's the airport road pipeline and then just a connection over to Pony Express.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:15.520
 Our well 9 is right here east of the airport, and that'll actually connect in to

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:16.520
 this.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:20.520
 And so this pipeline will not just be our Central Utah pipeline, but it will connect into

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:21.520
 our well system, too.

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:22.520
 So we have the redundancy.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:27.520
 Do we have the right of ways and easements for this connection right here?

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:33.520
 So we have the piece on the airport already with the church.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:38.520
 All we have is permission to drill on that site, and we've talked to them.

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:42.520
 They're master planning their site right now, and they want us to run that pipeline along

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:46.520
 their roadways, and they don't know where those roadways are going to be yet.

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:50.520
 And so we're in communication with them to be able to do that in the future.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:57.520
 And so as we do the design, we'll take that into account.

00:20:57.520 --> 00:20:58.520
 Okay.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:20:58.520
 Jumping to wastewater.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:20:59.520
 Again, these are all projects.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:00.520
 Oh, yeah.

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:01.520
 Go for it.

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:02.520
 Okay.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:02.520
 Jumping to wastewater.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:03.520
 Again, these are all projects.

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:04.520
 Oh, yeah.

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:05.520
 Go for it.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:08.400
 Jumping to wastewater. Again, these are all projects.

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:10.200
 Oh, yeah. Go for it.

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:12.900
 Okay.

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:14.620
 I got a question.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:19.600
 The very last lot.

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:21.720
 The well nine.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:28.940
 So, you know that very bottom road that goes north-south, that last lot to the north on the west side.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:30.960
 It looks south from your house, you'll see it.

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:37.400
 Okay.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:46.720
 Wastewater projects. These ones are ones we're currently working on, and we're going to continue to ask funding to move forward.

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:58.560
 The ox ditch upgrade is really the first phase of our wastewater treatment plant, and it's increasing the capacity of our current process.

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:11.060
 The oxidation ditch is really a biological process where we have bacteria that helps take out the chemicals and everything else that's in our wastewater.

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:26.200
 And as part of this process, there's going to be a recycling system put in, and we're going to use another one of the existing tanks on site to kind of expand the capacity of what's already there.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:39.560
 And that really just is kind of a band-aid to get us to the timeline that we need to finish the full build-out of the 8 million or the 6 million gallon membrane bioreactor.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:44.820
 Will we continue to use it or utilize its capacity even after the new plant is built?

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:45.180
 Okay.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:45.980
 Yep, yep.

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:51.340
 And so we'll have two different processes running at the same time, and that's part of the full plan.

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:59.960
 There could be a time where we decide to retire that and use that land for something different, but that would be 20-plus years in the future.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:07.900
 Yeah, but I suspect that will happen if we have additional capacity where we can create that affluent water and cease to send it into the ox ditch, right?

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:19.180
 Yeah, our master plan, I think, has a full build-out flow capacity of like 14 million gallons, and so this is just our first step to get us up to about 8 million gallons.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:29.760
 And then, yeah, the 6 million gallon MBR wastewater treatment plant.

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:32.500
 The membrane bioreactor is newer technology.

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:46.100
 You can put it in a smaller space, and the effluent that comes out of it is a much better effluent, better quality, and it's also more usable as far as reuse systems and things like that.

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:57.900
 We don't have to have so many tertiary or other treatments that happen after the fact to get it to a point where we can reuse it for watering lawns and gardens and our parks and things like that.

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.660
 But, yeah, that's going to be a big chunk this year.

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:06.260
 That's the estimate of what we will spend just this year.

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:12.560
 And then the East Trunk Line at 5.9 million.

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:24.080
 So, just to get you oriented on this one, this is Pony Express, kind of coming down, right down the middle here.

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:28.060
 This is Airport Road, where it ties into Pony Express.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:34.080
 Now, where we were talking, the end of that 16-inch line is actually right there, right in the corner.

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:44.780
 And so, we're going to connect onto that with our East Trunk Line, run it down the rest of Airport Road, and then our East Expressway comes down this way.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:47.620
 And we'll use a lot of that right-of-way where we can.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:51.160
 The elevations don't allow us to use that whole right-of-way.

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:55.440
 We'll have to kind of jump down here and go into some of the developments.

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:58.140
 We'll obviously keep it in roadways and things like that.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:03.320
 But that trunk line will come down all the way south along that power corridor.

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:07.800
 And then we'll jump it to the west over to our treatment plant.

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:13.900
 Now, the purpose of this is we have a trunk line right now in Pony Express.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:22.500
 That one, level of service-wise, is about a C at this point, which is at the top end of acceptable.

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:31.900
 And so, if we continue to just tie in different developments and commercial or residential developments into that, it's going to be an issue pretty quickly.

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:39.620
 And that leaves the groundwork, really, for that Smiths development to come in and to utilize and also for the City Hall area to develop effectively, right?

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:40.240
 Correct.

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:40.880
 Yep.

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:42.160
 Yep.

00:25:44.740 --> 00:25:50.980
 So, the green areas in this map are everything that will drain to this east trunk line.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:26:03.140
 And this actually connects into that airport road, which drains the whole northwest side of the city, eventually, up even by the junior high and across SR73.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:29.180
 That also, as we take that flow that's currently coming down Pony Express and bypass it basically around into our east trunk line, that opens up additional capacity and drops our level of service down from a C back to a B or so and gives us extra capacity in that Pony Express pipeline so we're not right at the top end where we want to be.

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:37.720
 And really, most of the stuff in that line, at least, that feeds into that is kind of built out, right?

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:38.900
 Probably a little bit of hidden hollow.

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:42.700
 Otherwise, that should last us.

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:44.500
 It should last us.

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:47.780
 Yeah, there are some small areas right around where the Smiths are.

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:51.560
 There's kind of some open area there, which will actually go back to Pony Express.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:27:04.360
 And so, yeah, it should, the plan is to have east trunk line taken off that we don't have to upsize anything in Pony Express to be able to do full build out.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.660
 There's a lot of one that goes on the east side that will carry almost that development, right?

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:08.480
 Some of it.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:08.740
 Yeah.

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:10.020
 Yep, that's right.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:15.080
 Okay.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:24.060
 These are our new projects that we're proposing for this year.

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:30.860
 Effluent disposal upgrades as we grow and as we push more water towards the treatment plant.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:34.080
 That just means there's more output from the treatment plant.

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:40.280
 And we have to have a way to either reuse or dispose of that.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:45.960
 And so, this one, we're looking at different options for disposal.

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:52.040
 We're already kind of talking to some engineers as to what our options could be there.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:57.940
 But this would include full design of that.

00:27:57.940 --> 00:28:03.140
 But it would also include land acquisition, depending on which option we choose to do.

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:05.640
 And really, we're looking at three different options.

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:15.660
 We can run a pipeline from the end of our wastewater treatment plant, pump it up to the Tickville wash, and then run it down the Tickville wash.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:20.820
 There's some things that we have to talk to Saratoga Springs about.

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:23.880
 Obviously, it flows through them and things we have to consider there.

00:28:24.260 --> 00:28:25.040
 But that's an option.

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:33.720
 The second option would be to get it down to the sinks and then just let it kind of naturally spread.

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:37.020
 Right out where the water kind of collected there in the past.

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:39.320
 There's wetlands areas down there.

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:42.540
 And we would just be increasing the size of those wetlands, obviously.

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:46.680
 That would be land acquisition and talking to property owners and things like that to make that happen, too.

00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:51.580
 Well, you may be getting there.

00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:56.900
 I was just thinking about the conversation we've had with, is it Mount Airy on their lift station?

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:05.620
 I know there's like a process that we're kind of working through, but I'm just wondering if we should account for some budget.

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:10.480
 Or we have, was it eight lift stations throughout the city?

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:11.540
 Yeah.

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:13.400
 So, what's that?

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:15.000
 Scenic Mountain.

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:15.580
 Scenic Mountain, thank you.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:23.840
 I don't know if we've accounted for those situations or if we should consider that as a council.

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:27.720
 It sounds like this is probably the time to be thinking about that.

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:42.220
 I've got a different question for you, and it's really, it's a philosophical question where we're looking at dumping money into these disposal upgrades, right?

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:47.180
 Are we just limited because the state won't allow us to reuse that water?

00:29:47.180 --> 00:29:55.500
 Because to me, it seems more pertinent and wiser for us to be putting that money into system upgrades that will accept a secondary water source.

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:56.580
 Yes, yes.

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:57.900
 And that's absolutely true.

00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:07.220
 Right now, our disposal is rapid infiltration basins, which are basically big ponds, and we're hoping that the water just goes into the groundwater.

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:20.300
 Because we live in a valley that's lined with clay, that happens very slowly, and so we have, we really have an immediate need to expand our capacity.

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:23.980
 So, when you're talking about disposal upgrades, are you talking about making some additional ribs?

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:25.120
 Is that what I'm hearing you say?

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:27.180
 So, that would be an option, too.

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:27.960
 Yep.

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:28.580
 Yep.

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:36.560
 That would be land acquisition, and that's probably the more expensive and less efficient way to do it because of the clay that we live on.

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:49.340
 Now, back to your reuse question, yeah, absolutely, we would love to be able to reuse all the water, and in fact, we've done upgrades to our treatment plant to upgrade the quality of our effluence so that we can reuse it.

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:53.000
 But, yeah, it's held up at the state at this point.

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:55.840
 We can't get permitted to do that yet.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:31:07.200
 I get that we have to plan for what our situation is now without that approval, but part of me feels like a one-pager or a fact sheet or something.

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:25.720
 Or something could be created with this dollar, this fiscal impact, and the way it would be without being able to use reuse, and the way it would be with reuse, and share that with our partners on the state level that have advocated for that permit for us.

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:37.280
 Because how much longer are we going to really spend $10 million because we don't have the reuse permit yet?

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:39.620
 That seems really silly.

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:43.020
 Not that our staff or anyone's doing it.

00:31:43.080 --> 00:31:46.980
 It just seems like the state is putting us through an undue burden.

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:54.200
 No, but, I mean, you're reading my mind exactly, Council Member Clark, and that's my concern is, at what point are we going, I mean, they want us to be water-wise and efficient.

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:57.160
 At what point are they going to help us be that?

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:04.180
 Well, and ribs, I mean, you talk about evaporation, like, doesn't water evaporate into the air if we put it in a rib first?

00:32:04.460 --> 00:32:04.840
 It does.

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:09.040
 Yeah, so, like, that's just as bad as, like, watering lawns and stuff like that.

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:11.260
 I get that it's after it's treated and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:13.320
 But we're losing water.

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:17.420
 It's totally different than when we can put it back into the system.

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:21.220
 Yeah, absolutely on the same page, yeah.

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:29.280
 And maybe this is a greater conversation with strategic and legislative affairs, but I totally get what you're saying.

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:37.240
 It's like, how much more are we going to burden our taxpayers with the delays that the state's causing?

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:40.700
 Well, I mean, this comes from wastewater impact fees.

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:45.980
 So the developers are paying for this, and I just think there's a more effective way to put that water to use.

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:52.580
 Then we could send that one-page fact sheet to the Home Builders Association and let them also write letters to the state.

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:56.540
 I mean, for me, that's all I'm wondering is, isn't there a more efficient way to do this?

00:32:56.560 --> 00:33:03.560
 And I think the answer is yes, but we're prohibited to do anything effective about it today, and we need to deal with it.

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:06.220
 I'm sure you've thought about this, Vince.

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:09.720
 Have you thought about anything outside of the box that's different than this?

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:16.660
 Is there something else that we can do in the hope that this is a temporary problem, not a permanent solution that we have to come up with?

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:23.540
 Right, right, and I think that would be the RIB solution just to expand the capacity we have to store by site.

00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:27.680
 Can we build bigger berms on our RIBs and just funnel it all down there and just take longer to percolate?

00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:28.780
 I mean, I don't know the answer.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:29.660
 I'm not a water guy.

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:30.160
 Right.

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:35.180
 But the more I learn about this, the more I realize that there's a lot that we can be doing that we're not doing.

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:46.780
 So the most logical step would be not to do RIBs or to put the water into a particular wash, because both will evaporate.

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:57.760
 And the reason that the state is not overly excited about reuse is because the reuse is used in the secondary system for which it would evaporate when you put it in people's yards and whatnot.

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:16.900
 So short of using it for other uses that we're trying to figure out and come together with, another alternative might be to increase the water quality even one step further, which makes it culinary grade, and then direct inject that into the aquifer itself.

00:34:17.140 --> 00:34:27.220
 You can't direct inject if it's a type 2, type 1 water, you still have to make it culinary grade, but by direct inject, you don't have the evaporation and at least would improve our aquifer.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.220
 Have we considered the cost differential?

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:43.640
 Because I'm going to tell you, if we're sitting here looking at $7 million, would that money, that's the question in my mind, would that money be better spent in investing in a system that would allow us to do something that's more effective with that water?

00:34:44.140 --> 00:34:53.260
 So what we have to do in order to have the project go forward is we have to have the ability to say in our plan what we're going to use as the water, where the water will go.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:35:01.380
 And the state is the one that's really pushing us away from the RABs, which are not effective because of our clay layer, to the tickle wash.

00:35:01.380 --> 00:35:16.280
 We do have grave concern that when you put that water into the tickle wash, we'll be changing the environmental nature of that wash, thereby greening it up, adding the potential for endangered species or whatever we might be doing.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:23.020
 Once we change the environment and we start adding water to that, then getting water away from that could be a negative impact as well.

00:35:23.020 --> 00:35:32.440
 And so time is of the essence, and that's why we're meeting with the state engineer's office and the Department of Water Quality, because we would much rather reuse the water in an appropriate method.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:40.900
 But yet our plan still has to have a way to get rid of the sewer fluid, and if we don't, we don't get the permit, and we can't process our sewer.

00:35:40.900 --> 00:35:50.060
 So we have to jump through the hoops, and we're following their direction at this point, but we are working concurrently to try to change the politics of it as well.

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:53.380
 But if that changes, then we would not spend this money, correct?

00:35:54.520 --> 00:35:58.220
 It's all the timing and the approvals and whatnot, but yes, that's our goal.

00:35:59.060 --> 00:36:14.480
 So I mean, I would say put it in a tick to a wash, at least threaten that, because something that I wanted everyone here to know, especially on this end, these guys I think were in those conversations, but I met with Sandal Saturday, and he's kind of the water guy up at the state.

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:19.780
 I told him right now our stuff's just evaporating currently, and he's like, what are you talking about?

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:28.880
 And I said, you know, we can get this to the lake much quicker, and so now he's also on board, and I think that we, I mean, I wouldn't be in favor.

00:36:29.380 --> 00:36:36.180
 We need to have a plan, but I definitely wouldn't pull the trigger on that, knowing that we just need to keep pressuring them.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:43.440
 What is the timeline, Vince, where we're going to be compelled to have to do something with this additional water discharge?

00:36:44.560 --> 00:37:02.340
 So the initial study would take probably three to six months, depending on the option that we choose, and then after that, it would be property acquisition if we decide to go that way, and I think at that point, that's where we're really saying this is absolutely where we're going to go with it if we start buying property.

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:06.900
 And so four to six months, I said, I would say is where we have some flexibility.

00:37:08.200 --> 00:37:11.940
 Mayor, I hope you can get in front of the state and go advocate.

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:18.320
 I know you've been doing that, but I'm hopeful that maybe we can get something to change where we might be able to pursue a different course of action.

00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:20.500
 But they're not in session, so even if we...

00:37:20.500 --> 00:37:22.140
 I mean, I guess they could approve the permit, right?

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:22.580
 Yes.

00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:25.800
 So, and that's where it really comes out.

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:36.200
 We have to get the sewer processing permit approved, and you can't get the permit approved without saying how you're going to use the water, even though we might be working on another way to dispose of the water.

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:46.820
 So are you suggesting, Ben, the best way to approach it is to approve this in the budget so that it recognizes that there is a plan to take care of it, but then to go to the powers that be and say, help us do something better?

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:57.760
 Absolutely, and the reason being is we can do exactly what Councilmember Clark just mentioned a few minutes ago, which is we now have a story to share of money that's wasted with our state legislators.

00:37:58.420 --> 00:37:59.220
 Okay, that's perfect.

00:37:59.380 --> 00:37:59.900
 I like that.

00:38:02.560 --> 00:38:03.840
 But not waste it, right?

00:38:04.760 --> 00:38:07.060
 I don't like the wasted money, but I like the plan.

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:19.940
 Yes, yes, that we want to invest the money properly so that we can truly reuse that water and create a return on it and have an ever-growing water resource as our sewer processing continues to grow, as our community continues to grow.

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:26.220
 Yep.

00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:41.200
 So, Councilmember Wright, your idea of having other options, the next project is really kind of another option and another step.

00:38:41.340 --> 00:38:42.760
 I did, yep.

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:56.500
 So, this tertiary treatment phase two, reverse osmosis, is the one that, reverse osmosis really takes the salinity of our effluent and bumps that number way down.

00:38:56.500 --> 00:39:01.700
 Because that's the number that really is preventing us from just injecting it right back into our aquifer.

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:12.460
 If we inject high salinity water into the aquifer, eventually it will infiltrate and we'll be getting high salinity water into our wells, and we just, we want to avoid that.

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:25.840
 And so, in order to make that process happen, we have to have a reverse osmosis piece added to, it's another treatment train that would be added to our existing train that we already do at the wastewater treatment site.

00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:49.560
 Now, if we decide to not do direct injection and go with a different effluent, this would not be a wasted study or a wasted number, because this is also the next step to be able to make our effluent better for watering our parks and our yards and things like that.

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:57.100
 And it's really the next step in direct potable reuse also, which years down the road, we may have to go to that.

00:39:58.100 --> 00:40:06.600
 Just for clarification, the 300,000 that's listed under the tertiary treatment phase two reverse osmosis, is that to implement it or to study it?

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:07.080
 It is not.

00:40:07.620 --> 00:40:07.940
 Nope.

00:40:08.460 --> 00:40:08.700
 Nope.

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:15.680
 That's just an initial study in engineering design to figure out how we could fit that onto our existing train.

00:40:15.680 --> 00:40:19.320
 I just feel like I got the wrong degree at college.

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:27.440
 Okay.

00:40:28.340 --> 00:40:30.080
 Steeplechase lift station upgrade.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:32.160
 This is one.

00:40:32.500 --> 00:40:37.120
 The steeplechase lift station is basically the lift station that's down by Mehta.

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:47.880
 It's clear on the south end of the city, which is not too far from our wastewater treatment plant, but it's downhill enough that we can't get gravity flow into our current wastewater treatment plant.

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:54.300
 And so there is an existing lift station there right now.

00:40:54.300 --> 00:41:06.100
 And this would, with Mehta's expansion and with Williams going in down there, it's just a study and a design to determine what needs to happen to be able to take care of their effluent.

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:17.860
 Now, on their end, they're working with SOMAS engineering to kind of look at what their future demands are going to be on that lift station.

00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:23.880
 And so we'll be working closely with them on that project to determine what that upgrade is going to look like.

00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:28.500
 But it's more than likely upgrade of pumps, upgrade of electrical systems, things like that.

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:41.460
 I am just wondering if the city will be able to capture the cost of this study and any potential upgrades in the fees for this one particular user.

00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:44.540
 Yeah, that's a good question.

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:47.480
 I don't know, Kimberly, if you had a thought on that.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:52.920
 I missed the very beginning part of that.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:55.040
 Okay, so you're fine, Kimberly.

00:41:55.180 --> 00:41:56.280
 I know you're pulling stuff together.

00:41:56.660 --> 00:41:59.080
 It's the steeplechase lift station upgrade.

00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:14.220
 And what I'm asking is, will the city be able to capture this cost and any cost for any additional upgrades from this one additional user or this one user with fees?

00:42:14.220 --> 00:42:21.480
 This is the one where the city is going to, just looking at, basically, it's been a private lift station and now the city is going to be.

00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:22.860
 No, this is the one that's over by Meta.

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:25.340
 This is the city lift station, yeah.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:26.600
 It's down by Meta.

00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:37.700
 Yeah, so in terms of, we haven't had, how about I just, there hasn't been any conversation about that at this point.

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:41.480
 Do we have the ability to recoup fees?

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:42.320
 Yes.

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:48.360
 But only if you start breaking out your rates into certain fee areas, depending on the users that are in there.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:55.400
 And we wouldn't be able to just single out, well, we just want this one lift station and we're going to assess only them.

00:42:55.400 --> 00:43:02.420
 Any other lift station that then the city has, we would need to make sure that we're looking at that regionally and then you're choosing to break those rates up.

00:43:02.860 --> 00:43:04.240
 Even for the data center?

00:43:04.580 --> 00:43:06.740
 When it's very specifically for the data center?

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:18.800
 There are, well, we could look into a little bit further, but there are certain terms in their agreements about our rates that say specifically that we can't do things that target this type of industry specifically.

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:33.000
 And Marcus might not, but that was what, when I had done my research that they had had some information that was like, we can do rates, but we can't make rate changes that are targeted only for data centers.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:50.360
 So we'd have to do a rate change that's relevant to the industrial users of the city, or you could say for lift stations, we want to have everybody who is being serviced by a lift station that we're going to start assessing a lift station fee as part of their wastewater billings.

00:43:50.680 --> 00:44:05.240
 I would like for us as a city to look at ways to capture those funds back because we've had great robust discussions as a body about whether or not we're willing to take on a lift station responsibility for 120 homeowners.

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:10.620
 But we can't ask for those funds to be included.

00:44:10.620 --> 00:44:14.080
 There's got to be something in the development agreement or something else.

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:21.800
 I just don't see us subsidizing a business with dollar amounts when they're the only ones using that lift station.

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:29.340
 I think that's really the question is, does the development agreement require them to pay for this?

00:44:29.420 --> 00:44:32.400
 And if it doesn't, then my next question would simply be this.

00:44:32.800 --> 00:44:45.040
 As we look at any future expansion or any other requests that they bring, I think we need to be conscientious of these things and make sure that it's contained in a new amendment to a development agreement.

00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:46.960
 I agree with Council Member Clark.

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:54.680
 There's no reason for the citizen to bear the burden of increasing, of studying and paying for an upgrade to a sewer lift station that services one company.

00:44:54.900 --> 00:44:56.960
 I just, I think fundamentally that's wrong.

00:44:57.520 --> 00:45:09.200
 Now they may have paid wastewater impact fees of which we would be able to utilize impact fee funds to be able to do an upgrade if it's increasing capacity that is for that purpose.

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:12.580
 Those would be the funds that they would have already paid that we would be able to utilize.

00:45:12.580 --> 00:45:14.160
 They would have paid the impact fee funds?

00:45:14.680 --> 00:45:43.000
 So if, what the original agreement was, so if this is the original data centers, there are certain fees that, anyway, I don't know if we collected them all up front or not, but if there were impact fees that were paid, regardless if it's them, right, if it's a system upgrade, then, and the lift stations are there, and it's enhancing it, we would be able to use impact fees for that only if it's increasing capacity to the lift station.

00:45:43.000 --> 00:45:53.800
 Well, Mayor, maybe as a council, and I don't know if the council supports us or not, maybe we could just ask you to bring us the, to have someone research and bring us the data that helps us understand the impacts that we're dealing with here.

00:45:53.900 --> 00:45:54.580
 What's the history?

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:56.460
 What's the historical context?

00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:07.880
 It would just be good to review it, because, frankly, if we didn't consider this, as we took a look at the Williams approval, then we missed it, and I think we need to learn from that mistake.

00:46:08.980 --> 00:46:12.320
 So, it sounds like a couple of things need to happen.

00:46:12.460 --> 00:46:16.960
 I'm going to need some help from Marcus to review the contract and Vince to help us understand that.

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:21.060
 So, do you guys want to put something together that, and anyone else that might?

00:46:21.880 --> 00:46:27.020
 Yeah, I'm not as familiar as what this upgrade is intended to accomplish, so it's hard to say.

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:39.060
 If it is to facilitate the expansion for meta, then that is something that is baked into the development agreement for us to meet and confer and assess, you know,

00:46:39.560 --> 00:46:42.420
 what portion they need to pay for things like that.

00:46:42.780 --> 00:46:47.100
 If it's for existing for some reason, then that will be governed separately.

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:55.900
 But if it's for the expansion, then this is something that is baked into the agreement, because all of the infrastructure upgrades at the time we did that development agreement were not known.

00:46:56.500 --> 00:47:00.720
 And so, we put in a provision that we'd come back and look at what they need to do.

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:02.760
 That's the same data I'm looking for, Marcus.

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:05.680
 That is something that was considered when we did the development agreement.

00:47:06.320 --> 00:47:09.220
 So, it sounds like this could be something that they should have to pay for anyway.

00:47:09.400 --> 00:47:10.120
 So, we need to get...

00:47:10.120 --> 00:47:15.600
 I would certainly think that if it is only being done because of their expansion, then yes.

00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:22.740
 So, before we fund this, we need to make sure that we get that information and bring it back, if that's okay.

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:27.320
 And as Kimberly alluded to, there's multiple ways to fund it.

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:30.060
 It could be they just pay for it directly.

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:32.540
 It could be they pay impact fees that pays for it.

00:47:32.760 --> 00:47:35.060
 There's various methods of accomplishing the same goal.

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:35.880
 As long as we make sure.

00:47:36.300 --> 00:47:39.240
 Yeah, but the goal is that, hey, if it's for the...

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:42.600
 If it's being necessitated by their expansion, then they should pay for it.

00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:44.560
 So, if you get that back to us.

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:47.640
 We got another budget meeting coming in a couple weeks, I presume.

00:47:48.020 --> 00:47:49.780
 So, just stay on top of that.

00:47:50.080 --> 00:47:50.360
 If you would.

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:51.820
 Sounds good.

00:47:55.160 --> 00:47:55.600
 Okay.

00:47:55.600 --> 00:47:55.600
 Okay.

00:47:55.600 --> 00:48:03.100
 So, the last project on the list here is our effluent pond storage liner replacement.

00:48:03.980 --> 00:48:08.420
 We just talked about RIBs where we're trying to get rid of water and get water to go into the ground.

00:48:08.940 --> 00:48:18.320
 We have two ponds out there that are currently our return water from Metta that we actually do use in our secondary system.

00:48:18.320 --> 00:48:21.880
 And that water, we do want to save as much of it as we can.

00:48:21.980 --> 00:48:24.160
 And so, we have two ponds that are lined.

00:48:24.840 --> 00:48:27.140
 Those liners over the years have worn.

00:48:27.720 --> 00:48:32.020
 And with weather and storms and things that have come through, they've ripped up pretty good.

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:35.280
 And so, we're at a point where we need to replace those liners.

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:36.860
 They're beyond their useful life.

00:48:38.120 --> 00:48:44.580
 And so, that will be the $4 million.

00:48:46.220 --> 00:48:49.040
 So, any questions on that one?

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:53.620
 I do have one question on all of these.

00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:55.300
 Is there a reason?

00:48:55.540 --> 00:48:58.440
 Are these just like good round numbers?

00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:04.720
 I get a little worried when I just see round numbers like give or take a million.

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:05.100
 Right.

00:49:05.180 --> 00:49:07.120
 How close are we to the actual cost?

00:49:07.640 --> 00:49:08.820
 Is it going to be my question?

00:49:08.980 --> 00:49:10.540
 These are probably within...

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:12.560
 Like, what's the standard deviation of where we're going to actually land?

00:49:12.560 --> 00:49:13.960
 Probably 25%.

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:14.840
 Oh, okay.

00:49:15.040 --> 00:49:15.280
 Yep.

00:49:15.400 --> 00:49:16.720
 Or within 25%.

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:20.140
 Until we get into the actual RFP process.

00:49:20.280 --> 00:49:24.140
 I think it's important to understand that this is our staff saying, hey, these are the things.

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:25.400
 And this is what we think it could cost.

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:26.520
 But we have not gone to bid.

00:49:26.700 --> 00:49:27.800
 We don't have a design.

00:49:28.340 --> 00:49:35.700
 So, he's trying to let us, when he says 25%, obviously, we could bid it and be higher or lower.

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:37.160
 And that's more my question.

00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:40.960
 So, my question, like, are we budgeting over 25%?

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:46.120
 So, our intent, and these numbers do have contingencies in them for the most part.

00:49:46.300 --> 00:49:50.660
 And so, the hope is that this is the high end and it will come down.

00:49:50.920 --> 00:49:52.840
 It doesn't always end up that way, but that's our hope.

00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:53.800
 I'm going to hold you to it.

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:54.660
 No, I'm just kidding.

00:49:54.920 --> 00:49:55.420
 Thank you.

00:49:56.300 --> 00:50:03.460
 And what it does for us on the finance side is we're looking at our reserves and where we're at, and then we start committing.

00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:03.980
 Right?

00:50:04.040 --> 00:50:16.480
 We're saying this much is being killed off and we have to commit it for this project because we're starting it and moving forward to make sure that we will have the cash flow and the funds available at the time when that project comes forward.

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:21.640
 And any funds not used stay in the capital project fund for future projects?

00:50:21.640 --> 00:50:26.320
 I think that's it for me.

00:50:26.440 --> 00:50:28.660
 Oh, stormwater is the next one.

00:50:30.100 --> 00:50:45.900
 In our stormwater request for this year, we only have one project, and this is a project that was in our last stormwater master plan that we did in 2000, I think it was 2020 when we did that plan.

00:50:47.900 --> 00:50:50.500
 What this is, is bank reinforcement.

00:50:50.820 --> 00:51:03.060
 There's areas in that plan where it shows that weathering of the bank, which is a natural process, but it's getting close to people's backyards and structures and things like that.

00:51:03.060 --> 00:51:21.320
 And so the plan is to go in and reinforce those banks with either large riprap or gabion baskets, which are kind of the wired baskets that are rock-filled to keep those banks in place and to protect property from damage.

00:51:24.460 --> 00:51:40.740
 Because we don't have a biologist on staff or an environmental planner on staff currently, is there going to be some sort of review to make sure that we're not destroying critical habitat and shoring up the issues that we're concerned about?

00:51:41.240 --> 00:51:43.720
 Yeah, that will be part of the design process.

00:51:43.880 --> 00:51:48.720
 As we get into the design, we'll hire an engineer out, and they will look at that as part of the process.

00:51:48.820 --> 00:51:49.620
 Okay, thank you.

00:51:52.500 --> 00:51:56.520
 I'm curious if any of this has been done over in the Silver Lake area already.

00:51:56.720 --> 00:51:58.840
 I mean, Field's a flagship.

00:51:59.080 --> 00:52:02.360
 Flagship did a lot of work down in there already.

00:52:02.960 --> 00:52:03.120
 Yes.

00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:06.740
 And I know they had to work with the Army Corps of Engineers to get a permit, and it was very specific.

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:14.100
 Is this something that we're coming in and repairing that they did, or is it something that they didn't do that they should have done?

00:52:14.680 --> 00:52:15.440
 Northridge.

00:52:17.280 --> 00:52:19.980
 The one on the right side is Silver Lake.

00:52:20.300 --> 00:52:21.960
 Yeah, so there's kind of two different areas.

00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:24.060
 And, yeah, go ahead.

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:27.800
 This does not include any of the work that Flagship did.

00:52:27.920 --> 00:52:33.600
 This is, when you're looking at the Silver Lake area, this is more specific to the Silver Lake Village side.

00:52:35.280 --> 00:52:40.060
 That has some serious erosion where the homes are pretty close.

00:52:41.060 --> 00:52:45.020
 And when it comes to the wash, right, we don't want a straight wash that shoots through.

00:52:46.280 --> 00:52:53.620
 But in some of these areas, the wash has gone completely the other direction, and it kind of ping-pongs back and forth, right?

00:52:53.680 --> 00:52:56.700
 And when it ping-pongs, that's where it makes these cuts to the wall.

00:52:57.120 --> 00:52:59.320
 I don't take any issue with the need to do it.

00:52:59.380 --> 00:53:04.620
 My concern really was, are we repairing something that wasn't done correctly, and it doesn't sound like that's the case.

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:13.440
 We've already done some BDA work and gabing basket work upstream of where they did their work.

00:53:14.040 --> 00:53:16.480
 And this is work that needs to take place downstream.

00:53:17.060 --> 00:53:27.080
 And some of that is trying to place BDAs so that we can slow the water down and try to actually utilize more of the wash, right?

00:53:27.100 --> 00:53:33.360
 Some of this wash is incredibly wide, but when you get down to the ditch, right, it's four or five feet wide.

00:53:34.000 --> 00:53:40.480
 And so we want to try to spread some of that and straighten it just a little bit so it meanders instead of ping-pongs.

00:53:40.840 --> 00:53:44.260
 And then we need things like gabing baskets and stuff just to shore it up.

00:53:44.260 --> 00:53:48.420
 And frankly, Zach, I mean, you're raising a point that was concerning to me when we were talking about the wastewater.

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:55.940
 If we're going to dump that affluent into the Tickbill Gulf, we're only going to have these types of mitigation problems that we're going to have to deal with regularly.

00:53:56.380 --> 00:53:57.940
 So it's not free to do that.

00:53:58.220 --> 00:53:58.360
 Yeah.

00:53:58.900 --> 00:53:59.100
 Okay.

00:53:59.540 --> 00:54:01.240
 Just a quick question before you walk away.

00:54:01.440 --> 00:54:09.320
 Did we fix the wash wall where we had the water main leak off of Silver Creek shot through between the houses?

00:54:09.780 --> 00:54:10.100
 Yes.

00:54:10.100 --> 00:54:12.600
 So we've already solved that wash wall?

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:12.840
 Yeah.

00:54:12.840 --> 00:54:16.440
 I believe that's – I believe so.

00:54:16.760 --> 00:54:20.760
 I guess I can't say 100%, but I believe that was all done.

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:21.440
 Yeah, because I know that was compromised as a result of that.

00:54:22.180 --> 00:54:24.100
 Yeah, which isn't part of this.

00:54:24.240 --> 00:54:26.240
 This is more of –

00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:27.160
 Right.

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:30.740
 While we're there, though, let's make sure that that part has been eroded.

00:54:30.740 --> 00:54:31.060
 Okay, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:31.340
 Thanks.

00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:35.060
 Yeah.

00:54:35.060 --> 00:54:35.960
 Any other questions?

00:54:37.400 --> 00:54:42.960
 Just going back to something you said earlier, you said the church is master planning the area north of Lone Tree.

00:54:43.380 --> 00:54:43.500
 Yep.

00:54:43.500 --> 00:54:46.420
 And I'm just curious, is this the first time we're hearing this as a council?

00:54:47.200 --> 00:54:47.460
 Yes.

00:54:47.660 --> 00:54:54.700
 And the reason why I say that is landowners really should come to the council and be a little bit more collaborative, in my opinion.

00:54:54.700 --> 00:54:59.900
 And so if they want a master plan, we're not – apparently we're not being involved in that thought process.

00:55:00.420 --> 00:55:07.200
 And I would really prefer landowners and developers to come to us first, and then we can start to have a collaborative discussion from the beginning,

00:55:07.520 --> 00:55:12.220
 instead of them deciding what they're going to do, say, well, this is what we're going to do, and then come to us.

00:55:12.260 --> 00:55:14.340
 Now, we want them to come to us early in the process.

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:20.960
 So I get a little frustrated when they're off planning something and they haven't even talked to us.

00:55:21.600 --> 00:55:27.940
 And so I don't know how we remedy that, but if – I mean, I would assume the council would agree with me on this issue.

00:55:28.600 --> 00:55:35.660
 Well, I think the answer is developers have found if they don't collaborate, they get a very quick no.

00:55:36.120 --> 00:55:39.880
 So I think that's what's there.

00:55:40.060 --> 00:55:47.120
 This is one that I've mentioned to the body that would be coming and that I was nervous on our general plan that if we left as agricultural,

00:55:47.300 --> 00:55:52.620
 it might leave an unknown what is it that we really want.

00:55:52.800 --> 00:55:56.640
 So I've mentioned that I knew they were working on it, but nobody's seen anything.

00:55:56.640 --> 00:56:00.360
 And really, if they don't come to us, that's their fault if they're going to know.

00:56:01.140 --> 00:56:02.780
 How do we know they're working on it then?

00:56:03.060 --> 00:56:03.700
 Nobody's seen it.

00:56:06.980 --> 00:56:08.020
 What was the question?

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:12.980
 Well, that's – he brought it to us before, and he's like, oh, we need to leave it as agriculture.

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:15.700
 But, I mean, somebody's seen something.

00:56:15.980 --> 00:56:17.340
 We know that something is happening.

00:56:17.500 --> 00:56:20.080
 Why don't we just bring it to us and say, let's talk about it?

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:21.920
 Because it's not a small piece of property.

00:56:22.300 --> 00:56:22.800
 It's huge.

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:29.180
 And it's going to have an absolute impact on everything around it, depending on how they decide to develop it.

00:56:29.240 --> 00:56:34.940
 So I just – with the very likelihood of creating schools from it, that's how big it is.

00:56:35.260 --> 00:56:36.700
 So I just – I agree with Craig.

00:56:36.820 --> 00:56:38.180
 I think we need to push them to bring it in.

00:56:38.220 --> 00:56:39.340
 I don't care what stage they're at.

00:56:39.400 --> 00:56:39.980
 Let's have a chat.

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:48.500
 Have them come forward in a work session or have them meet with two or three of the mayor and two council members, I think, honestly.

00:56:49.380 --> 00:56:51.800
 Is this all the way from Lone Tree up to Sage Valley?

00:56:52.180 --> 00:56:52.480
 Yes.

00:56:52.720 --> 00:56:53.400
 That's all their property.

00:56:53.740 --> 00:56:57.000
 Well, I know they own all of that, but the part that's being considered –

00:56:57.000 --> 00:56:58.460
 That's the master plan is the whole property.

00:56:58.460 --> 00:56:58.740
 Okay.

00:56:58.980 --> 00:56:59.220
 Yeah.

00:56:59.360 --> 00:56:59.540
 Yeah.

00:56:59.600 --> 00:57:01.180
 The zoning all around that is –

00:57:01.180 --> 00:57:04.920
 And, again, I want to reiterate, I don't think anybody knows what they're planning.

00:57:05.160 --> 00:57:12.040
 It's only been said to the planning commission – sorry, not planning commission – the planning staff that, hey, by the way, we're looking at this area.

00:57:12.140 --> 00:57:13.140
 That means nothing.

00:57:13.960 --> 00:57:16.280
 And so there is no stage of development.

00:57:16.780 --> 00:57:20.120
 What it is is we know that in their mind they're thinking about something.

00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:24.280
 If they don't collaborate, then they're going to get likely known.

00:57:24.780 --> 00:57:27.220
 I don't think they're as far along as you might think they are.

00:57:28.100 --> 00:57:29.360
 It's up to them.

00:57:29.780 --> 00:57:33.720
 You know, I mean, I'm not calling everybody saying, hey, by the way, can you come to work session, tell us what you're doing?

00:57:34.180 --> 00:57:36.380
 It's up to them to initiate the call.

00:57:36.780 --> 00:57:42.860
 So maybe we need to train the planning staff, because they're probably sort of at the tip of the spear, is my guess,

00:57:43.260 --> 00:57:49.640
 that if they're hearing stuff like this, that their trained response to these developers would be, hey, have you talked to the city council members yet?

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:52.060
 Have you sort of floated by them what you're wanting to do?

00:57:52.140 --> 00:57:54.260
 Have you met with them to see what the city's looking for?

00:57:54.860 --> 00:57:58.300
 Maybe have them sort of plant that bug in the ears of the landowners?

00:57:58.300 --> 00:58:09.140
 And my guess is that is what happens, and they're just not quite there, meaning they're in the very preliminary stages of discussion, but that is how we treat it, is my belief.

00:58:09.320 --> 00:58:11.660
 So why would we be concerned about the agriculture, then?

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:14.980
 If they're not very far down the road, why would we even have a concern about agriculture?

00:58:16.400 --> 00:58:21.820
 I just exploded out there as my own personal belief that I think with no direction, there's no direction.

00:58:22.020 --> 00:58:27.320
 And I wanted to indicate exactly that my belief is it should be bigger lots.

00:58:27.800 --> 00:58:32.200
 That way they know what it is that we're looking for up front as they come forward.

00:58:32.780 --> 00:58:37.440
 If you think that agriculture is fine, then I think that's a fight we're going to have to fight.

00:58:37.440 --> 00:58:38.720
 I'm just going to go one step further.

00:58:38.840 --> 00:58:42.860
 I think what Craig's saying is we need to reach out to the church and say we'd like to talk about this parcel.

00:58:44.020 --> 00:58:46.620
 I mean, the reality is I think that's where we're at.

00:58:46.620 --> 00:58:54.040
 It's too big to wait for them to come to their own conclusions, work with other entities, school district as an example of that,

00:58:54.120 --> 00:58:58.800
 and make decisions that we're not even, haven't been apprised to, and then we have to live with that decision.

00:58:59.440 --> 00:59:00.460
 Nobody's made a decision.

00:59:00.580 --> 00:59:01.280
 That's my whole point.

00:59:01.480 --> 00:59:02.180
 The church, nobody.

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:03.580
 There's no decision in every decision.

00:59:03.580 --> 00:59:04.360
 Jared, I'm not saying that.

00:59:04.460 --> 00:59:10.360
 I'm saying we reach out to the representatives from the church and say we'd like to talk about your piece of property here.

00:59:10.840 --> 00:59:11.060
 Yeah.

00:59:14.880 --> 00:59:16.020
 I can certainly do that.

00:59:16.020 --> 00:59:21.160
 We actually met with the church today, this afternoon, and had a meeting about other properties,

00:59:21.520 --> 00:59:25.980
 but that's the first I had heard that they were designing, that they were starting to master plan.

00:59:26.520 --> 00:59:32.880
 We'll reach back out to them and request a meeting, or at least request either a work session or request some individual meetings.

00:59:33.280 --> 00:59:37.660
 It might be even easier in a private, like a, not that we're trying to keep something out of the public,

00:59:37.660 --> 00:59:42.940
 but like just the mayor and two council members, because that's easier to brainstorm outside the public eye.

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:45.680
 I volunteer as tribute.

00:59:46.020 --> 00:59:46.840
 Thank you for that meeting.

00:59:50.720 --> 00:59:51.080
 Okay.

00:59:52.080 --> 00:59:53.080
 And that's all I had.

00:59:53.360 --> 00:59:56.720
 I'll turn it over to Mr. Hilton to do the rest.

00:59:57.580 --> 00:59:59.540
 Okay, just real quick.

00:59:59.680 --> 01:00:02.580
 Can we just thank, can we just thank Ben for proactive?

01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:03.140
 Thank you.

01:00:03.140 --> 01:00:03.460
 Thank you.

01:00:05.540 --> 01:00:06.160
 Great work.

01:00:06.480 --> 01:00:12.620
 And before we go on, I probably should have done this earlier, but I just hadn't put into motion because I didn't know what it was going to fit.

01:00:13.100 --> 01:00:23.840
 But I meant to mention Zach's birthday today, and our awesome staff brought birthday cake, but I didn't tell everybody to grab one, so Natalie's been over there cutting that for us.

01:00:23.840 --> 01:00:37.320
 And I'm not a singer, but I know we've got a hard stop in one hour, so I just asked her to cut it, and as we go on, maybe bring a piece up to each one of the council members and the staff, and we'll say happy birthday to Zach.

01:00:37.440 --> 01:00:38.540
 So that's why that's over there.

01:00:38.540 --> 01:00:42.380
 Thank you, Mayor, and thank you, everyone, whoever prepared that.

01:00:42.460 --> 01:00:43.420
 We do not need to sing.

01:00:43.500 --> 01:00:47.420
 We can roll on with parks, have a piece of cake, and continue.

01:00:48.260 --> 01:00:48.880
 Thank you.

01:00:49.100 --> 01:00:57.740
 If you want to bring those up, and we'll just, maybe if we keep our mouths full, we'll likely get through a little bit more.

01:00:58.180 --> 01:01:00.100
 Yeah, I was just going to say good timing, Mayor.

01:01:00.200 --> 01:01:00.440
 Thanks.

01:01:00.520 --> 01:01:02.500
 I'd prefer to do mine while you guys are all eating.

01:01:03.840 --> 01:01:08.040
 So, good evening.

01:01:08.540 --> 01:01:10.780
 Good afternoon, Council and Mayor.

01:01:11.400 --> 01:01:14.860
 I'm going to do the parks portion of these projects.

01:01:17.020 --> 01:01:31.800
 I'm going to start with the list of projects that we are currently in, in several different stages, and I want to talk about some of these, just most of these projects will need to roll over into the next budget year.

01:01:32.940 --> 01:01:43.320
 The first one is the trail from Pony Express Parkway to SR73, which is a MAG project that we're receiving funding for.

01:01:43.320 --> 01:01:59.340
 This project goes from Pony Express, basically where the park and ride is located at, and we'll go up and tie in by 73 up by the Scenic Mountain subdivision.

01:02:01.180 --> 01:02:12.340
 So, we are being held up on funding for this project, it is approved, but they use some of the money that was originally meant to go to this, some federal funding, so they're reallocating some money.

01:02:13.180 --> 01:02:17.700
 So, we're just waiting for the approval on that to move forward.

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:21.260
 Zach, can I just jump in really quick?

01:02:24.560 --> 01:02:29.160
 I do want to just clarify on that, you're not one with the ranches trail and stuff.

01:02:29.500 --> 01:02:35.520
 So, that is the estimated cost of the project, but the city's cost will, it's only around 6% or something.

01:02:35.720 --> 01:02:46.800
 Yeah, that we will have to make on that, and we have that portion allocated, I can pull it up, but that is the full estimated project cost.

01:02:52.720 --> 01:03:09.460
 The second one is the observatory, which is, we're still trying to receive more donations for.

01:03:09.460 --> 01:03:21.560
 I do know that staff recently just did a presentation to the county, and I understand that Metta is possibly looking at doing some extra donations.

01:03:22.220 --> 01:03:24.340
 I just was going to ask, have we reached out to QTS?

01:03:24.780 --> 01:03:28.700
 They're looking for some opportunities to do something in the community, this might be something they're looking for.

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:29.760
 I do not know.

01:03:30.160 --> 01:03:38.520
 I mean, I know when they very first came, that was one of their objectives, to not just come build the data center, but to actively invest in the community.

01:03:38.700 --> 01:03:40.780
 This might be a good opportunity as a kickoff for them.

01:03:41.000 --> 01:03:42.460
 Yeah, I don't know that.

01:03:42.840 --> 01:03:51.720
 So, I think where Metta started off in this project, and we're working through the county to try to acquire grant funds right now, we're looking to Metta to

01:03:51.720 --> 01:03:57.960
 finish off this project, and maybe other opportunities for QTS, since this one's kind of there.

01:03:57.960 --> 01:04:07.900
 The third is the Eagle Monument, which is well on its way.

01:04:08.820 --> 01:04:12.820
 Eagle Mountain staff has already been out there and rebuilt the retaining wall.

01:04:13.880 --> 01:04:17.260
 The contractor has been out there, has poured the footings and the pad.

01:04:18.400 --> 01:04:23.160
 Right now, the schedule date is to have this installed around the end of May.

01:04:24.140 --> 01:04:28.900
 So, this is one project we should be able to complete without rolling over to the next year.

01:04:31.700 --> 01:04:34.400
 Well, I'll cue it in just real quick.

01:04:34.520 --> 01:04:39.780
 I'll throw out the original estimate was between $40,000 and maybe $50,000.

01:04:40.240 --> 01:04:43.480
 Is this an additional $80,000 or an additional $30,000 or what's the...

01:04:43.480 --> 01:04:46.600
 No, the approved amount is $80,000.

01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:51.620
 This is what's approved, and it's going to be pretty close to this.

01:04:51.620 --> 01:04:52.760
 Including the budget amendment from before?

01:04:54.840 --> 01:04:55.660
 Because we didn't...

01:04:55.660 --> 01:05:02.680
 No, we never actually allocated or approved any money until the last budget amendment.

01:05:03.100 --> 01:05:05.600
 And that's when we allocated the $80,000.

01:05:06.680 --> 01:05:11.060
 And that was based off of design that was approved and cost.

01:05:12.260 --> 01:05:13.640
 As well as the retaining wall.

01:05:14.120 --> 01:05:14.760
 So, maybe...

01:05:14.760 --> 01:05:15.400
 Yes, yes.

01:05:15.560 --> 01:05:17.360
 That's the sign versus the wall that we did there.

01:05:18.040 --> 01:05:19.600
 Yeah, there's...

01:05:19.600 --> 01:05:21.380
 We had to rebuild the retaining wall.

01:05:22.920 --> 01:05:25.920
 So, it feels like we approved this twice is what I'm getting at.

01:05:25.940 --> 01:05:27.660
 Didn't we already approve this in a budget amendment?

01:05:27.860 --> 01:05:28.360
 It's already out.

01:05:29.540 --> 01:05:34.520
 All these projects that I'm going over right now are already funded projects.

01:05:34.760 --> 01:05:39.860
 It's just that a good portion of these are going to need to roll over into the 27 year.

01:05:41.120 --> 01:05:43.680
 This one is one we should be able to complete, right?

01:05:44.080 --> 01:05:46.460
 But most of these projects will need to roll over.

01:05:47.360 --> 01:05:52.720
 I think the confusion is because we just have that one header at the top that says additional budget requested.

01:05:53.380 --> 01:05:56.800
 These projects are already allocated.

01:05:56.800 --> 01:05:57.800
 Yes, yep.

01:05:58.120 --> 01:06:01.220
 There's funding for all these ones that I'm going over first.

01:06:03.560 --> 01:06:06.560
 Zach, timing on the parking lot expansion at Cory Ride?

01:06:07.560 --> 01:06:09.120
 So, we have...

01:06:09.120 --> 01:06:10.120
 Well...

01:06:10.120 --> 01:06:15.920
 We have designed for the parking lot right now.

01:06:16.580 --> 01:06:23.400
 The goal is to have a contractor and be building that hopefully this summer.

01:06:24.680 --> 01:06:30.220
 This is a project that will certainly be rolled over into the next year, but we want to start construction on it this season.

01:06:31.440 --> 01:06:44.380
 I'm just wondering, with some of the big events like 4th of July and some of the bigger ones, obviously not Pony Express days, but will we be able to accommodate more people?

01:06:44.680 --> 01:06:46.320
 And that's why I was asking about the time, so...

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:49.780
 The parking lot won't be finished for those.

01:06:49.780 --> 01:06:56.000
 It will probably be more likely that we'll have to coordinate with contractors on blackout dates, that they're not allowed to work.

01:06:57.680 --> 01:07:02.660
 And the one before that is actually the festival-filled lights, which is also a quarry ride.

01:07:04.460 --> 01:07:08.580
 We were really trying to have these done for the events this year.

01:07:11.440 --> 01:07:13.480
 It's still a maybe, but not likely.

01:07:13.920 --> 01:07:17.340
 We're just waiting on materials to have them in place.

01:07:17.480 --> 01:07:25.660
 This one will likely still be done before the end of the budget season, but unfortunately we probably won't have it for the Pony Express days events.

01:07:28.240 --> 01:07:36.840
 The last one on this page is the digital sign, which was approved at the budget amendment as well on the last budget amendment.

01:07:39.300 --> 01:07:46.240
 This one, staff will work with the contractor to come up with what we think is the appropriate design.

01:07:46.800 --> 01:07:55.380
 And this one will still come back to Council for approval, because I think there still needs to be discussion about the size of the digital sign.

01:07:58.240 --> 01:08:07.900
 Next, we have the cemetery master plan, which we haven't started on yet.

01:08:07.980 --> 01:08:12.580
 This year we took an ambitious goal of doing all master plans.

01:08:14.280 --> 01:08:18.760
 And so this is just the last one that we need to get going on.

01:08:18.820 --> 01:08:22.660
 So this one will also be one that we'll roll over into the 27.

01:08:24.800 --> 01:08:28.180
 The next one is the city center streetscapes.

01:08:28.880 --> 01:08:32.380
 This is funding that was from the utility cells.

01:08:33.660 --> 01:08:39.560
 And we've pushed this for quite a few years due to construction.

01:08:40.640 --> 01:08:42.060
 A ton of utilities.

01:08:43.800 --> 01:08:47.200
 There's two fiber lines going down that road right now.

01:08:50.600 --> 01:08:52.380
 I think you should ask that question.

01:08:53.020 --> 01:08:57.600
 The question I just leaned over now, Council Member Clark, is there even still a need to do it?

01:08:57.740 --> 01:08:59.080
 We're getting ready to rip these out.

01:08:59.080 --> 01:09:01.640
 So to clarify, there are two.

01:09:02.440 --> 01:09:05.960
 The city center streetscapes is the side of the street.

01:09:06.340 --> 01:09:11.880
 There is another $500,000 allocated for the medians, which is a different discussion that we do not.

01:09:11.880 --> 01:09:15.240
 The medians we should do, the medians we should just draft that as well.

01:09:15.240 --> 01:09:16.160
 Absolutely agree.

01:09:16.420 --> 01:09:18.980
 And that's not on that list for that reason.

01:09:20.500 --> 01:09:28.000
 The streetscape side, which is the shoulder, I think it's time we need to consider doing a bunch of that,

01:09:28.100 --> 01:09:33.220
 especially where we have moved the parade route this year.

01:09:34.520 --> 01:09:41.880
 I think that means in this next year, we really need to hash out what that's going to look like and try to get that done.

01:09:42.320 --> 01:09:45.380
 I think to your point, this is always a moving target.

01:09:45.500 --> 01:09:50.880
 For instance, the new approval with the commercial project is going to widen that to three lanes.

01:09:51.520 --> 01:09:58.760
 And so, even on the shoulders, I was looking for David because I think a lot of Pawnee Express could be...

01:09:58.760 --> 01:09:59.560
 He took off.

01:09:59.660 --> 01:10:00.200
 I know he had to leave.

01:10:00.440 --> 01:10:01.820
 But what I would say is this.

01:10:01.920 --> 01:10:07.360
 I mean, my suggestion would be we definitely should focus on those city center streetscapes.

01:10:07.420 --> 01:10:08.880
 I agree with that 100%.

01:10:08.880 --> 01:10:15.540
 And if we need to pull some of the budget out of those medians, if we need to take that $500,000 and dedicate it to doing more of the city center...

01:10:15.540 --> 01:10:16.600
 And that may be that...

01:10:16.600 --> 01:10:18.500
 I absolutely would be in favor of that.

01:10:18.500 --> 01:10:24.460
 So, I don't know how everyone else is going to feel about that, but I think we ought to do what we can to doll those things up real quick.

01:10:24.860 --> 01:10:27.660
 And answer, I guess, some of your comment, Mayor.

01:10:27.840 --> 01:10:32.880
 It's obviously something we would work closely with David to, so that we're not just putting it in and tearing it out.

01:10:35.900 --> 01:10:39.920
 Next is the Mountain Ranch Bike Park Edition.

01:10:42.800 --> 01:10:44.720
 That will...

01:10:44.720 --> 01:10:46.560
 This will need to roll over as well.

01:10:46.640 --> 01:10:49.640
 But the intent on this one is to go out for design.

01:10:50.780 --> 01:11:12.860
 This would be specifically targeting the parcel that we have between Golden Eagle and Ranches Parkway that is underneath the power line corridor to provide, like, the intent is to do, like, some parking, provide a parking spot and do maybe some more, like, beginner-level pump track type of stuff.

01:11:15.080 --> 01:11:26.280
 And then, lastly, for current projects, we have the Nolan Park improvements where we have money allocated to make some improvements to Nolan Park.

01:11:30.220 --> 01:11:34.360
 You know, we've had a number of people come in and ask about improving this.

01:11:34.360 --> 01:11:35.700
 It could very well.

01:11:36.060 --> 01:11:44.200
 I think some of the intent is to focus around the splash pad as well, because the splash pad is a non-recycling amenity.

01:11:45.220 --> 01:11:49.260
 That water pretty much is just wasted where our other one is recycled.

01:11:49.620 --> 01:11:50.420
 So, I'm going to loop it, then.

01:11:50.420 --> 01:11:50.920
 So, I'm going to loop it, then.

01:11:50.920 --> 01:11:51.260
 Okay.

01:11:51.260 --> 01:11:52.080
 So...

01:11:52.080 --> 01:11:52.760
 And that...

01:11:52.760 --> 01:11:54.620
 This is just for design and plan?

01:11:54.720 --> 01:11:57.000
 There's not actually anything that's happening with the 750?

01:11:57.480 --> 01:11:57.880
 No.

01:11:58.000 --> 01:12:01.440
 There's enough there that that would have construction with it, too.

01:12:01.820 --> 01:12:03.580
 I would assume probably not enough.

01:12:03.700 --> 01:12:05.380
 It just depends on what the design is, right?

01:12:05.560 --> 01:12:05.780
 Okay.

01:12:06.180 --> 01:12:06.480
 Thank you.

01:12:06.540 --> 01:12:09.820
 In fact, this might be the wrong time, but I'm just going to plant this seed.

01:12:09.880 --> 01:12:14.820
 I really would like to have a discussion as a council regarding parks, and what are the...

01:12:14.820 --> 01:12:16.740
 I mean, what are the next targeted...

01:12:16.740 --> 01:12:19.520
 And you guys probably already know this, and I should know this.

01:12:19.980 --> 01:12:23.440
 What are the next targeted big community or...

01:12:23.440 --> 01:12:25.940
 I always forget the terminology that you use.

01:12:26.560 --> 01:12:31.660
 Yeah, the region or the community-sized parks that we're targeting for improvement with impact fees.

01:12:31.660 --> 01:12:35.100
 I don't want to have that conversation right now, because I don't know that this is the appropriate time.

01:12:35.540 --> 01:12:37.500
 But I think it would be nice to say...

01:12:37.500 --> 01:12:40.320
 To understand what the planning is, where we're trying to program.

01:12:40.740 --> 01:12:43.900
 This park is next, and this one's next on the list, and this one's next on the list.

01:12:44.200 --> 01:12:46.520
 I think my next slide will hit that a little bit for you.

01:12:46.920 --> 01:12:48.440
 Look, you anticipated it.

01:12:48.480 --> 01:12:48.900
 Good work.

01:12:50.600 --> 01:12:55.340
 That brings us to some of our requests for the 27 budget.

01:12:57.280 --> 01:13:05.160
 That we would like to have $150,000 for the Pony Express Regional Park.

01:13:05.520 --> 01:13:16.900
 To do a design for the Pony Express Park, as well as $150,000 for the Silver Lake Community Park.

01:13:18.360 --> 01:13:20.160
 Which is in...

01:13:20.160 --> 01:13:21.220
 It's in Silver Lake.

01:13:21.300 --> 01:13:21.960
 There's already a portion.

01:13:22.060 --> 01:13:22.360
 I don't know.

01:13:23.520 --> 01:13:29.040
 It's right there by Tickville Wash that kind of includes that L-shape that we could complete that park.

01:13:30.900 --> 01:13:32.900
 We're talking about east of the amphitheater, correct?

01:13:33.860 --> 01:13:34.480
 For Silver Lake.

01:13:34.680 --> 01:13:35.780
 This is like...

01:13:35.780 --> 01:13:38.880
 No, it's south of the amphitheater.

01:13:39.060 --> 01:13:42.320
 It's down by Tickville Wash, right off of Silver Lake.

01:13:43.400 --> 01:13:46.560
 I think the road that ties into it is called Silver Parkway.

01:13:47.080 --> 01:13:49.640
 Is this the Woodhaven Park, or is this right?

01:13:53.140 --> 01:13:54.100
 Yeah, it's...

01:13:54.100 --> 01:13:55.740
 I think this is the Woodhaven one, right?

01:13:55.860 --> 01:13:58.780
 On the east by the elementary school, north of the Wash?

01:13:59.000 --> 01:14:01.520
 It's north, yes.

01:14:01.620 --> 01:14:03.160
 North of the elementary school.

01:14:03.820 --> 01:14:06.780
 It's right on the north side of Tickville Wash, yes.

01:14:06.900 --> 01:14:10.660
 We have two detention ponds right in the undeveloped area and everything.

01:14:10.880 --> 01:14:14.380
 It kind of makes it an L-shape to complete that park.

01:14:14.540 --> 01:14:18.420
 Do we have a plan to complete the Silver Lake Amphitheater Park?

01:14:20.400 --> 01:14:20.880
 No.

01:14:25.100 --> 01:14:28.020
 We already had that park designed.

01:14:28.020 --> 01:14:31.580
 Like, we paid for it, and there was a Phase B as part of that.

01:14:31.680 --> 01:14:34.000
 Is that not attainable anymore?

01:14:34.240 --> 01:14:35.960
 Couldn't we just use what was done?

01:14:36.540 --> 01:14:37.760
 I'd have to look into that.

01:14:39.160 --> 01:14:42.280
 Colby, Curtis, and I were both on the steering committee for that,

01:14:42.700 --> 01:14:45.460
 and I know that we asked them to program that in

01:14:45.460 --> 01:14:48.300
 because it was supposed to be a part of regional connectivity

01:14:48.300 --> 01:14:51.400
 with the trails along Tickville Wash, too,

01:14:51.480 --> 01:14:53.580
 and that was all part of the design.

01:14:54.240 --> 01:14:55.580
 I'll have to look into that.

01:14:55.580 --> 01:14:55.980
 Okay.

01:14:57.460 --> 01:15:00.080
 And lastly is the property acquisition,

01:15:00.540 --> 01:15:05.360
 which is actually not an expenditure for the next year,

01:15:05.560 --> 01:15:08.660
 but it's just allocating those funds.

01:15:08.840 --> 01:15:15.680
 We did an additional $1 million last year

01:15:15.680 --> 01:15:19.020
 with the intent that in 2028

01:15:19.020 --> 01:15:25.480
 that we have the $3 million to actually go out and acquire land

01:15:25.480 --> 01:15:29.640
 to do some improvements on some of our bigger parks.

01:15:30.900 --> 01:15:35.200
 We've kind of focused on this $3 million in 2028

01:15:35.200 --> 01:15:39.240
 with the mindset of doing another quad for baseball.

01:15:44.700 --> 01:15:53.840
 So I'm still concerned about, and I know we've talked about this briefly in the past,

01:15:53.920 --> 01:15:59.840
 but the areas in city center where there's all those undeveloped open space areas,

01:16:00.040 --> 01:16:03.280
 and it seems like we've got to get that on the plan somewhere

01:16:03.280 --> 01:16:05.780
 because it seems to me like there's still no plan, right?

01:16:06.500 --> 01:16:08.680
 And how long is there going to be no plan for those areas?

01:16:09.820 --> 01:16:12.540
 I think that goes to Council Member Wright's point

01:16:12.540 --> 01:16:16.640
 about having a discussion about what's the next thing

01:16:16.640 --> 01:16:18.440
 that we're going to do and what we're going to prioritize

01:16:18.440 --> 01:16:21.000
 because I feel like the list that we have right here

01:16:21.000 --> 01:16:24.860
 are all the things that we decided already to program

01:16:24.860 --> 01:16:29.480
 and that they were approved way prior to this

01:16:29.480 --> 01:16:32.720
 and that they're cleaning those things up and wrapping those up

01:16:32.720 --> 01:16:36.500
 and now it's on us to have a meeting specifically

01:16:36.500 --> 01:16:39.240
 about programming and planning the rest of it.

01:16:39.540 --> 01:16:41.920
 And I know it's just another plan,

01:16:41.920 --> 01:16:46.580
 but to me, you know, in the next council meeting,

01:16:46.700 --> 01:16:50.540
 we'll be bringing back our parks, trails, and open space master plan

01:16:50.540 --> 01:16:54.500
 that played kind of the first role in that,

01:16:54.600 --> 01:16:56.560
 and identifying our open space

01:16:56.560 --> 01:16:58.820
 and identifying what is undeveloped

01:16:58.820 --> 01:17:00.300
 because that's really what you're referring to

01:17:00.300 --> 01:17:02.440
 is the undeveloped areas and what we do with those.

01:17:03.480 --> 01:17:06.120
 But that master plan is a bit of a higher level.

01:17:07.040 --> 01:17:12.140
 And I think to me, the next step is identifying those undeveloped areas

01:17:12.140 --> 01:17:16.380
 and having an additional plan of what identifies those, right?

01:17:17.140 --> 01:17:22.960
 What are key components in those that are they things we want to develop?

01:17:23.140 --> 01:17:24.240
 What do they look like?

01:17:24.340 --> 01:17:27.300
 Are they land disposal?

01:17:28.000 --> 01:17:30.180
 Are they a hybrid of both, right?

01:17:30.180 --> 01:17:31.100
 We've done those even.

01:17:32.680 --> 01:17:35.760
 But to me, I think that's the next step after the parks, trails,

01:17:36.020 --> 01:17:40.440
 and open space master plan is actually having a plan specific to the undeveloped.

01:17:41.560 --> 01:17:44.780
 I feel like we need to have three separate meetings.

01:17:45.300 --> 01:17:46.640
 One, that we talk about trails.

01:17:47.080 --> 01:17:50.380
 That needs to be a comprehensive discussion about what we want our trails to be.

01:17:50.720 --> 01:17:54.560
 We probably need to treat it the same way that we treat our roads,

01:17:54.680 --> 01:17:58.820
 have level of trails with cutouts of exactly what that's going to look like.

01:17:59.360 --> 01:18:01.680
 So it's going to be an eight-foot or it's going to be a ten-foot.

01:18:01.760 --> 01:18:04.240
 It's going to be asphalt and it's going to have this on either side of it.

01:18:04.580 --> 01:18:06.740
 When we go to the next level of trail, it'll be this.

01:18:07.380 --> 01:18:09.660
 And then we need to do the same thing with our parks.

01:18:10.140 --> 01:18:14.960
 We need to literally bring in our regional parks and go through each of those steps and say,

01:18:15.040 --> 01:18:17.540
 okay, what is our plan with this?

01:18:18.540 --> 01:18:21.260
 What kind of funding mechanisms do we need to put in place?

01:18:21.420 --> 01:18:24.100
 So we're, to Craig's point, we're not just kicking them down the road.

01:18:24.340 --> 01:18:30.020
 And then we need to have one that's about the land that we own that is open space that is not improved

01:18:30.020 --> 01:18:33.520
 and have a discussion about how we're going to move forward with it.

01:18:33.620 --> 01:18:34.900
 Are we going to do something with it?

01:18:35.320 --> 01:18:36.660
 Are we going to sit on it for a while?

01:18:36.660 --> 01:18:42.140
 Or do we need to dispose of this property with a plan of disposal that makes it beneficial to the community?

01:18:42.220 --> 01:18:46.600
 But I think there's not enough time to have all three of those discussions in one meeting.

01:18:46.720 --> 01:18:52.740
 They need to be separated and then we need to have a discussion about a financial plan to deal with those things, in my opinion.

01:18:53.240 --> 01:18:55.220
 I actually support doing it that way.

01:18:55.380 --> 01:19:01.540
 I think sometimes we try to fit too much into a meeting and Councilmember Rowan, I think that's a perfect plan.

01:19:02.700 --> 01:19:07.480
 You're not, just to clarify, you're not referring to the master plan that's coming back.

01:19:07.620 --> 01:19:10.660
 I'm talking about the funding mechanism to take care of all three of those things.

01:19:10.940 --> 01:19:16.660
 Then your master plan actually makes sense because we have a plan in place to move forward with what that is.

01:19:16.760 --> 01:19:19.900
 And yes, there's going to be some additional elements to that that aren't in the master plan,

01:19:20.140 --> 01:19:21.680
 especially if we're doing cutaways of trails.

01:19:22.400 --> 01:19:26.020
 You know what section I'm talking about in the streets where it shows kind of the,

01:19:26.300 --> 01:19:30.800
 if I'm looking down the road for a major collector, minor collector, arterial,

01:19:31.400 --> 01:19:33.420
 this is the width of each of the roads.

01:19:33.740 --> 01:19:35.360
 It'll have a median or not have a median.

01:19:35.760 --> 01:19:38.640
 This is how much curb and gutter, here's the sidewalk and here's what's on the side.

01:19:38.920 --> 01:19:42.680
 I think we need to get to that level with our trails so that it's very clear.

01:19:42.840 --> 01:19:49.560
 And then our map needs to represent, when we're looking at it by color, it's an eight-foot asphalt path.

01:19:50.040 --> 01:19:54.180
 This is the main vein that everything else will come off of

01:19:54.180 --> 01:19:57.140
 and the next level will be a smaller level of trail system.

01:19:57.260 --> 01:19:58.040
 Maybe it's just dirt.

01:19:58.640 --> 01:20:02.180
 But that way it's at least clear to us how we're maintaining them

01:20:02.180 --> 01:20:05.460
 and where we need to be spending our focus of time and money on.

01:20:08.500 --> 01:20:13.460
 I personally struggle when I see that we've got $3 million allocated for purchase

01:20:13.460 --> 01:20:18.700
 when we have so much land that we could spend that $3 million on developing what we already have.

01:20:19.080 --> 01:20:24.880
 So just to be clear real quick too, that is impact fee money.

01:20:25.120 --> 01:20:31.320
 So it's not like we can take that money to go just develop any, it looks like Kimberly's.

01:20:31.660 --> 01:20:36.040
 And I think when you're talking too about the plan and what you're missing a little bit

01:20:36.040 --> 01:20:41.020
 is we currently have the Parks and Trails Impact Fee Facilities Plan, right?

01:20:41.080 --> 01:20:44.840
 So I believe that the first phase was working on the Master Plan.

01:20:45.340 --> 01:20:46.380
 Is that kind of how they work?

01:20:46.480 --> 01:20:47.640
 You do your Master Plan.

01:20:48.160 --> 01:20:51.960
 Then from your Master Plan, you move into the Impact Fee Facilities Plan

01:20:51.960 --> 01:21:00.940
 and then you move into the Impact Fee IFA, which is our funding source.

01:21:01.400 --> 01:21:06.220
 So I believe all that is tracking forward, but I think what it is

01:21:06.220 --> 01:21:11.200
 is that we've got to get through the Master Plan in order to move to the next phase,

01:21:11.880 --> 01:21:13.500
 which starts to get more granular.

01:21:14.060 --> 01:21:16.060
 So that Master Plan is that higher level.

01:21:16.560 --> 01:21:19.440
 Then we're going to get the IFFP that's going to get more granular.

01:21:20.520 --> 01:21:24.420
 And then from there, it converts to how much is this going to cost

01:21:24.420 --> 01:21:27.520
 and we have this funding mechanism that comes into place for the Impact Fee

01:21:27.520 --> 01:21:29.480
 and what we're going to try to spend.

01:21:29.760 --> 01:21:32.180
 Yeah, and there's going to be some restrictions on what you can spend

01:21:32.180 --> 01:21:34.920
 in the Impact Fee for parks, too, based off of acreage.

01:21:38.180 --> 01:21:41.100
 So I think this is a good opportunity to just plant this seed.

01:21:41.640 --> 01:21:44.120
 We can look at every single detail of every trail,

01:21:44.260 --> 01:21:47.580
 but one of the reasons some of our citizen boards haven't worked in the past

01:21:47.580 --> 01:21:49.120
 and I've sat on almost every one of them

01:21:49.120 --> 01:21:51.460
 is because they don't have a specific task.

01:21:52.420 --> 01:22:00.160
 I think this is one of those areas that specifically we could task with the Community Services Board

01:22:00.160 --> 01:22:04.600
 to do most of what you're talking about first and then get in.

01:22:04.840 --> 01:22:07.920
 And I agree the funding part is probably the next step.

01:22:08.340 --> 01:22:13.420
 But I think the first task that we as a body would want to say,

01:22:14.180 --> 01:22:16.820
 you know, I want the direction to come from the body is,

01:22:17.680 --> 01:22:18.760
 hey, go identify.

01:22:19.040 --> 01:22:21.780
 And I know Zach and I, even on Facebook, had some conversation.

01:22:23.040 --> 01:22:24.080
 Identify all of our parks.

01:22:24.260 --> 01:22:25.800
 Prioritize which ones are the most important.

01:22:25.880 --> 01:22:30.060
 Our trails, which ones are the most impactful, you know, in order.

01:22:30.340 --> 01:22:32.240
 And then we can start chipping away some funding.

01:22:32.460 --> 01:22:36.960
 But to me, that whole process, before we even start having multiple meetings,

01:22:37.120 --> 01:22:42.240
 is let's empower that board to locate every single park and trail,

01:22:42.480 --> 01:22:45.760
 prioritize each one, and then come to us with an idea

01:22:45.760 --> 01:22:48.260
 and then we can start allocating money as it comes in.

01:22:49.020 --> 01:22:51.400
 And expand on that idea if you wish.

01:22:51.980 --> 01:22:54.060
 But I would say that's where we could start

01:22:54.060 --> 01:22:56.120
 and just empower them to do that

01:22:56.120 --> 01:23:01.120
 before we go even another step forward on what you're saying is the plan, right?

01:23:01.480 --> 01:23:05.020
 Is that just the idea that I've had with the board?

01:23:05.140 --> 01:23:07.220
 I think Steve and I have talked and Zach and I for sure.

01:23:07.780 --> 01:23:10.060
 Is that what you guys want to do or a different direction?

01:23:12.740 --> 01:23:15.920
 I think you have to have instruction first for them to be able to act on.

01:23:16.640 --> 01:23:19.540
 So I think the community services board is exactly who should be doing it,

01:23:19.560 --> 01:23:21.320
 but we need to give them clear direction on what to do.

01:23:21.880 --> 01:23:23.920
 And they can bring that information back to us

01:23:23.920 --> 01:23:25.680
 and we can move forward with that.

01:23:25.680 --> 01:23:29.560
 But I think the master plan is only as good as your ability

01:23:29.560 --> 01:23:31.600
 to understand what it is and what its purpose is.

01:23:32.240 --> 01:23:35.820
 If it's just to show us what we have, it's not really a plan.

01:23:36.500 --> 01:23:37.060
 It's a map.

01:23:37.880 --> 01:23:40.480
 And I think that we need to have something in place

01:23:40.480 --> 01:23:43.560
 to set a financial plan in that master plan

01:23:43.560 --> 01:23:48.460
 of how we're going to move through each of those trails, parks,

01:23:49.100 --> 01:23:52.260
 starting from regional parks being the most important that we've identified.

01:23:52.460 --> 01:23:56.340
 But there's a lot of parks that we have half finished that need to be finished.

01:23:57.340 --> 01:24:01.620
 As part of that, we also need to talk about the paths that run along the right-of-ways

01:24:01.620 --> 01:24:02.500
 that we haven't completed.

01:24:02.800 --> 01:24:05.460
 So, I mean, there's some broader discussions,

01:24:05.680 --> 01:24:07.660
 but you can't eat the whole elephant at one time.

01:24:08.340 --> 01:24:10.500
 So, it's better to break it apart and say,

01:24:10.700 --> 01:24:12.460
 this discussion is going to be about parks.

01:24:13.000 --> 01:24:14.940
 This discussion is going to be about our open space.

01:24:15.100 --> 01:24:16.680
 This one's going to be about our trails.

01:24:16.840 --> 01:24:19.100
 We can give instruction at that point for the community services

01:24:19.100 --> 01:24:21.900
 to gather the information, bring their ideas back,

01:24:22.120 --> 01:24:24.140
 and then we can move on to those things.

01:24:25.260 --> 01:24:25.780
 Okay.

01:24:26.140 --> 01:24:26.840
 I agree.

01:24:27.000 --> 01:24:30.480
 I think we're meeting with the community services board tomorrow.

01:24:31.120 --> 01:24:34.860
 We've got a lot to get incepted and get everything rolling,

01:24:35.000 --> 01:24:37.080
 but that's certainly a task they can take on.

01:24:37.940 --> 01:24:42.180
 I commented on Facebook that we have our own asphalt machine,

01:24:42.900 --> 01:24:46.180
 and it's like having a baby grand piano, but you never play it.

01:24:46.920 --> 01:24:50.600
 I want to name that thing baby grand, and I want to see it going 365.

01:24:50.600 --> 01:24:54.120
 So, just to touch on that, right?

01:24:54.740 --> 01:24:59.080
 Well, and this is, because we do do a lot of projects,

01:24:59.160 --> 01:25:00.540
 and we do use our paver a lot,

01:25:01.300 --> 01:25:04.120
 but this is also the streets department does the paving.

01:25:04.340 --> 01:25:08.680
 So, when we're committing to doing trailways and parking lots

01:25:08.680 --> 01:25:13.300
 and things like that, being aware that we're using up a department

01:25:13.300 --> 01:25:16.920
 that has their own duties that's budgeted for something else, right?

01:25:16.920 --> 01:25:19.940
 It isn't like anyone can just put jump on a paver.

01:25:20.080 --> 01:25:21.540
 For employees, that's what it sounds like.

01:25:23.260 --> 01:25:25.860
 They're building a trailhead right now, right,

01:25:26.060 --> 01:25:28.280
 which is grant money that we received,

01:25:29.220 --> 01:25:32.020
 that basically we're building a parking lot and parking areas

01:25:32.020 --> 01:25:34.780
 and a turnaround that's pavement and concrete,

01:25:34.940 --> 01:25:36.740
 but it's the streets department building it.

01:25:37.060 --> 01:25:41.520
 And we use these a lot in places where we receive funding

01:25:41.520 --> 01:25:46.280
 just so that we can maximize that money instead of bidding it out.

01:25:46.880 --> 01:25:49.560
 Those are one of the ones we really like to do in-house.

01:25:49.560 --> 01:25:51.520
 So, we can maximize those free dollars.

01:25:52.720 --> 01:25:55.360
 And just to clarify, in your presentation,

01:25:55.560 --> 01:25:57.460
 the projects that you're putting forward,

01:25:57.980 --> 01:26:00.640
 those are coming from the general fund,

01:26:00.840 --> 01:26:04.940
 you're not anticipating any wrap tax dollars for these projects?

01:26:05.140 --> 01:26:06.420
 No, not on these.

01:26:06.480 --> 01:26:06.760
 Okay.

01:26:06.940 --> 01:26:07.860
 I just want to clarify.

01:26:08.020 --> 01:26:08.180
 No.

01:26:08.600 --> 01:26:11.980
 And I would just add one thing as far as when we talk about adding these trails

01:26:11.980 --> 01:26:13.640
 and things like that, just a friendly reminder,

01:26:14.480 --> 01:26:16.080
 and I know we talked about it a little bit,

01:26:16.140 --> 01:26:18.300
 is just remembering that it's,

01:26:19.460 --> 01:26:23.060
 when we develop these areas and we add trails and we add things like this,

01:26:23.560 --> 01:26:26.900
 it's a two-fold expenditure, right?

01:26:26.960 --> 01:26:27.960
 It could cost money.

01:26:28.440 --> 01:26:32.180
 We can't just go out and build them and then cheer and walk away.

01:26:32.420 --> 01:26:38.060
 Like, we need to be prepared to have a level of service that costs money to maintain those too,

01:26:38.160 --> 01:26:42.400
 whether it's trees or it's trails or it's undeveloped open space.

01:26:42.480 --> 01:26:43.620
 These are all things we have to,

01:26:43.660 --> 01:26:44.720
 if we're going to build them,

01:26:44.820 --> 01:26:46.780
 we have to commit to maintaining them.

01:26:48.060 --> 01:26:49.160
 So, agreed.

01:26:51.300 --> 01:26:52.860
 Just for a little clarity, Zach,

01:26:52.980 --> 01:26:56.500
 you said 365 paving can only happen early in the summer.

01:26:56.680 --> 01:26:57.640
 It has a certain temperature.

01:26:57.720 --> 01:26:59.000
 We're double-timing in the summer.

01:26:59.500 --> 01:26:59.900
 Okay.

01:27:00.080 --> 01:27:00.560
 Fair enough.

01:27:00.860 --> 01:27:01.620
 Ground the clock in.

01:27:02.240 --> 01:27:03.660
 But that's, it does get,

01:27:03.920 --> 01:27:04.800
 that thing gets used.

01:27:05.220 --> 01:27:05.620
 It does.

01:27:05.740 --> 01:27:07.880
 And like I say, just a reminder, right?

01:27:08.440 --> 01:27:13.240
 It is a whole different department that's capable of doing that,

01:27:13.320 --> 01:27:17.360
 that we have to be prepared to take away from them doing maintenance on roadways.

01:27:17.360 --> 01:27:18.540
 So, yep.

01:27:19.660 --> 01:27:21.440
 And then just to wrap,

01:27:21.540 --> 01:27:22.300
 I wanted to make sure,

01:27:22.380 --> 01:27:25.160
 and it's not necessarily for this because I know we're tight on time,

01:27:25.780 --> 01:27:28.860
 but is that something that we wanted to,

01:27:29.280 --> 01:27:30.940
 I'm not sure if we were saying the same thing,

01:27:31.040 --> 01:27:34.860
 are we going to task the Citizen Board,

01:27:35.560 --> 01:27:38.620
 Community Services Board to identify those projects

01:27:38.620 --> 01:27:40.760
 and prioritize them as far as a need?

01:27:40.880 --> 01:27:41.200
 No.

01:27:41.920 --> 01:27:44.280
 Explain to me what I have some thoughts about that, Mayor.

01:27:44.380 --> 01:27:44.880
 I don't think,

01:27:44.880 --> 01:27:47.920
 I don't think that the Citizen Board should be prioritizing that.

01:27:48.460 --> 01:27:51.520
 I think they can make a recommendation regarding prioritization if they'd like,

01:27:51.620 --> 01:27:52.880
 but what I'd like to see them do

01:27:52.880 --> 01:27:56.140
 is to identify the holes in each of those individual networks

01:27:56.140 --> 01:27:58.380
 and to identify where we're lacking

01:27:58.380 --> 01:28:01.200
 and where they'd like to see some emphasis placed

01:28:01.200 --> 01:28:04.560
 and then allow the council to take a look at that and prioritize it

01:28:04.560 --> 01:28:07.160
 because we're going to have to prioritize it based on budget constraints,

01:28:07.380 --> 01:28:09.380
 whether there's impact fees that are available,

01:28:09.580 --> 01:28:10.860
 whether it's grant money that's available,

01:28:11.040 --> 01:28:12.460
 whether we're going to use parks,

01:28:12.620 --> 01:28:13.940
 wrap tax money.

01:28:14.380 --> 01:28:16.720
 So, I think the prioritization needs to land with the council,

01:28:16.920 --> 01:28:17.780
 but I would love...

01:28:17.780 --> 01:28:19.200
 So, I said that...

01:28:19.200 --> 01:28:20.280
 I used a bad word.

01:28:20.360 --> 01:28:20.760
 I'm sorry,

01:28:20.840 --> 01:28:22.440
 because you and I are saying the same thing.

01:28:22.480 --> 01:28:22.880
 I'm not sure.

01:28:23.460 --> 01:28:25.140
 I'm asking what is their task?

01:28:25.640 --> 01:28:27.340
 And if that is to identify the park

01:28:27.340 --> 01:28:28.660
 and put into an order,

01:28:28.800 --> 01:28:29.920
 without using the word prioritize,

01:28:30.760 --> 01:28:33.800
 some needs of their recommendation,

01:28:34.560 --> 01:28:36.740
 and then the council would obviously have to look at that.

01:28:36.820 --> 01:28:37.820
 Is that the right task?

01:28:37.900 --> 01:28:38.080
 Yep.

01:28:38.460 --> 01:28:38.580
 Yep.

01:28:38.580 --> 01:28:40.920
 I think in a lot of ways,

01:28:41.080 --> 01:28:41.680
 the difference,

01:28:41.820 --> 01:28:43.340
 what I hear Council Member Wright saying,

01:28:43.740 --> 01:28:45.660
 is to really take stock of something

01:28:45.660 --> 01:28:48.700
 or to inventory what is deficient

01:28:48.700 --> 01:28:50.140
 and what needs improvement,

01:28:50.500 --> 01:28:52.980
 not prioritizing what needs improvement.

01:28:53.300 --> 01:28:53.780
 Agreed.

01:28:54.280 --> 01:28:56.980
 It needs to just be what needs to be done,

01:28:57.360 --> 01:28:58.940
 and then we can give priority for them

01:28:58.940 --> 01:28:59.920
 to go back and evaluate.

01:29:01.460 --> 01:29:03.720
 And there's plenty for them to assess.

01:29:04.880 --> 01:29:08.500
 So, I was trying to facilitate a project for them

01:29:08.500 --> 01:29:10.280
 based on Craig's question.

01:29:11.280 --> 01:29:15.040
 Zach and Melissa will be there tomorrow.

01:29:15.780 --> 01:29:17.660
 Are you in agreeance

01:29:17.660 --> 01:29:19.340
 and understand what the will of the body is

01:29:19.340 --> 01:29:21.420
 based on Craig's original question?

01:29:22.260 --> 01:29:24.540
 Identify the gaps that exist

01:29:24.540 --> 01:29:26.920
 in the current system that we have.

01:29:28.760 --> 01:29:31.320
 And making a list of future needs.

01:29:31.560 --> 01:29:32.660
 Or something else in open space.

01:29:32.960 --> 01:29:34.420
 Yeah, but I think each one of those

01:29:34.420 --> 01:29:36.220
 has its own individual gaps, right?

01:29:36.320 --> 01:29:36.880
 What is the gap?

01:29:37.140 --> 01:29:38.340
 I mean, what I would start with is,

01:29:38.420 --> 01:29:39.580
 what are the gaps in the trails?

01:29:40.200 --> 01:29:41.520
 What are the gaps in our parks?

01:29:42.180 --> 01:29:44.380
 Where are we missing a level of service?

01:29:46.160 --> 01:29:48.540
 I think we start with the most expensive one first.

01:29:49.700 --> 01:29:50.900
 Yeah, it's got to be the parks.

01:29:51.060 --> 01:29:52.440
 That's an asset that's declining

01:29:52.440 --> 01:29:54.560
 if we don't do something about them to improve them.

01:29:55.060 --> 01:29:56.800
 So, to me, what is the thing

01:29:56.800 --> 01:29:58.120
 that's the most treasured thing first?

01:29:58.180 --> 01:29:59.060
 It's got to be parks,

01:29:59.800 --> 01:30:01.660
 seconds, trails, open spaces, and improved.

01:30:01.760 --> 01:30:02.920
 That's got to be probably last.

01:30:03.260 --> 01:30:06.160
 But I think, yeah, sorry.

01:30:06.360 --> 01:30:08.060
 I agree with you, Council Member Wood.

01:30:08.100 --> 01:30:10.220
 I think that there is going to be some prioritization

01:30:10.220 --> 01:30:10.900
 that has to happen there.

01:30:12.140 --> 01:30:13.620
 But my personal feeling is,

01:30:13.700 --> 01:30:15.200
 I think they need to be working on

01:30:15.200 --> 01:30:16.680
 at least those two,

01:30:16.740 --> 01:30:18.260
 and perhaps all three simultaneously.

01:30:19.360 --> 01:30:21.360
 I'm just recognizing that there are gaps

01:30:21.360 --> 01:30:22.700
 in every one of those areas,

01:30:23.280 --> 01:30:25.280
 and then we're going to have to juggle

01:30:25.280 --> 01:30:27.780
 which ones we're going to get to first.

01:30:27.960 --> 01:30:29.240
 I think you've got seven people.

01:30:29.960 --> 01:30:31.280
 Let me come into subcommittees then.

01:30:32.020 --> 01:30:33.880
 There's plenty of people to take on some tasks

01:30:33.880 --> 01:30:34.940
 to go through those things.

01:30:35.940 --> 01:30:37.320
 I think we need to give them

01:30:37.320 --> 01:30:38.560
 a level of creativity, though.

01:30:38.640 --> 01:30:40.480
 I'd like to ask them to think creatively

01:30:40.480 --> 01:30:42.920
 and bring us things that are thinking outside the box

01:30:42.920 --> 01:30:44.740
 and not feel like that they're just doing

01:30:44.740 --> 01:30:46.760
 objective assignments,

01:30:46.860 --> 01:30:49.580
 but they can be very creative as well

01:30:49.580 --> 01:30:50.600
 and bring us ideas.

01:30:52.060 --> 01:30:54.720
 I agree that that eventually needs

01:30:54.720 --> 01:30:55.920
 to be part of the process,

01:30:56.160 --> 01:30:59.220
 but before anybody can get creative about anything,

01:30:59.400 --> 01:31:00.880
 I feel like we have to look at

01:31:00.880 --> 01:31:02.540
 what we're deficient in.

01:31:03.260 --> 01:31:04.820
 And I feel like that's what I hear

01:31:04.820 --> 01:31:06.000
 Council Member Wright saying

01:31:06.000 --> 01:31:08.380
 and Wood as well.

01:31:08.640 --> 01:31:11.140
 And I think Zach said, yes,

01:31:11.180 --> 01:31:12.020
 we're going to have them do

01:31:12.020 --> 01:31:13.400
 kind of an assessment of it.

01:31:13.940 --> 01:31:16.500
 But I think we need to look at

01:31:16.500 --> 01:31:18.480
 what's deficient,

01:31:18.720 --> 01:31:19.760
 how we're going to fund that,

01:31:19.880 --> 01:31:21.940
 and then give them the opportunity.

01:31:22.600 --> 01:31:24.140
 Okay, we want your creative ideas

01:31:24.140 --> 01:31:27.180
 for this park or this trail.

01:31:28.940 --> 01:31:30.580
 But I, yeah, go ahead.

01:31:30.580 --> 01:31:32.900
 I just want to get something

01:31:32.900 --> 01:31:34.340
 that I have been thinking about

01:31:34.340 --> 01:31:35.880
 that I would love the Council's input.

01:31:36.000 --> 01:31:37.100
 I'm sorry, Zach, to get it.

01:31:37.180 --> 01:31:37.920
 Do you have more slides?

01:31:38.620 --> 01:31:39.480
 No, I'm done.

01:31:39.700 --> 01:31:40.200
 Oh, you're done?

01:31:40.320 --> 01:31:41.060
 I'm just making sure

01:31:41.060 --> 01:31:42.180
 there's no questions for me.

01:31:42.340 --> 01:31:43.800
 We're off on a tangent.

01:31:44.320 --> 01:31:46.040
 We're like down the rabbit hole

01:31:46.040 --> 01:31:47.620
 going somewhere else for a second.

01:31:47.780 --> 01:31:49.980
 But the question that I wanted to ask

01:31:49.980 --> 01:31:50.660
 for the Council,

01:31:51.820 --> 01:31:54.380
 coming back to Council Member Whiting's

01:31:54.380 --> 01:31:56.540
 comment about them having creativity,

01:31:56.820 --> 01:31:58.480
 when we look at expenses,

01:31:59.840 --> 01:32:01.160
 we could just save the money

01:32:01.160 --> 01:32:03.100
 and compound it and grow it

01:32:03.100 --> 01:32:06.660
 and create a money printing machine over time.

01:32:07.100 --> 01:32:10.520
 We could use it to fund people's salaries

01:32:10.520 --> 01:32:12.940
 to go out and take care of the fields,

01:32:12.940 --> 01:32:15.400
 and then you eat up that perpetually,

01:32:15.540 --> 01:32:16.820
 that fund, every year.

01:32:17.020 --> 01:32:18.020
 You know, if you hire somebody,

01:32:18.460 --> 01:32:19.660
 let's say $70,000,

01:32:19.820 --> 01:32:21.420
 and you bring in $600,000,

01:32:21.500 --> 01:32:22.640
 there's 10% of your budget.

01:32:23.140 --> 01:32:26.560
 Or we could look at one-time big expenses

01:32:26.560 --> 01:32:27.600
 like putting in a trail.

01:32:27.760 --> 01:32:28.800
 You have to maintain it,

01:32:28.840 --> 01:32:29.800
 but maybe not as much

01:32:29.800 --> 01:32:31.620
 as putting the thing in initially.

01:32:31.800 --> 01:32:32.660
 And so I'm curious

01:32:32.660 --> 01:32:34.240
 on the Council's thoughts around

01:32:34.240 --> 01:32:37.160
 giving them a little bit of creativity

01:32:37.160 --> 01:32:38.420
 in these areas.

01:32:38.520 --> 01:32:39.400
 Are we looking for,

01:32:40.220 --> 01:32:41.940
 do we want to have a policy

01:32:41.940 --> 01:32:42.840
 where we're spending,

01:32:43.720 --> 01:32:44.860
 you know, money comes in

01:32:44.860 --> 01:32:46.060
 and then it goes back out?

01:32:46.260 --> 01:32:49.240
 Are we looking to keep some of it aside?

01:32:49.600 --> 01:32:51.380
 Are we looking for one-time expenses?

01:32:51.560 --> 01:32:53.220
 Are we trying to avoid those expenses

01:32:53.220 --> 01:32:56.080
 that are recurring heavy expenses year to year?

01:32:56.260 --> 01:32:57.320
 Does that make sense?

01:32:58.220 --> 01:33:00.240
 I think the financial side of it

01:33:00.240 --> 01:33:01.900
 is really something that we,

01:33:02.560 --> 01:33:03.720
 as the budget makers,

01:33:03.880 --> 01:33:05.300
 really have to come to terms with.

01:33:05.400 --> 01:33:07.040
 I think their role

01:33:07.040 --> 01:33:08.920
 is to bring us deficiencies

01:33:08.920 --> 01:33:11.040
 and when assigned,

01:33:11.280 --> 01:33:12.280
 bring us ideas

01:33:12.280 --> 01:33:14.880
 to make those deficiencies better

01:33:14.880 --> 01:33:16.200
 or to dream big

01:33:16.200 --> 01:33:17.380
 and have something different.

01:33:17.820 --> 01:33:19.660
 And I feel like that dollar question

01:33:19.660 --> 01:33:22.080
 and prioritizing all of that stuff

01:33:22.080 --> 01:33:25.020
 takes place in the three meetings

01:33:25.020 --> 01:33:27.640
 that Council Member Wood spoke about.

01:33:31.520 --> 01:33:32.660
 Maybe one other thing

01:33:32.660 --> 01:33:33.760
 to be thinking about too

01:33:33.760 --> 01:33:35.400
 is there are some different categories

01:33:35.400 --> 01:33:36.120
 in each of those, right?

01:33:37.040 --> 01:33:38.980
 perhaps we're underfunding our maintenance.

01:33:39.620 --> 01:33:40.620
 That's part of the problem

01:33:40.620 --> 01:33:42.220
 is that we're not being able

01:33:42.220 --> 01:33:42.900
 to keep up with it

01:33:42.900 --> 01:33:43.820
 because we're either undermanned

01:33:43.820 --> 01:33:44.860
 or underfunded or both.

01:33:45.880 --> 01:33:47.440
 And then if we're talking about

01:33:47.440 --> 01:33:48.720
 the completion of a park,

01:33:48.800 --> 01:33:49.900
 that's a whole separate issue

01:33:49.900 --> 01:33:51.200
 and a different funding mechanism.

01:33:52.740 --> 01:33:53.880
 There's a design element

01:33:53.880 --> 01:33:55.540
 that has to come into part of that.

01:33:57.280 --> 01:34:00.100
 to Jared's point about the time of the year.

01:34:00.100 --> 01:34:01.260
 If we're talking about trails

01:34:01.260 --> 01:34:02.360
 and laying asphalt,

01:34:02.720 --> 01:34:03.480
 that has to follow

01:34:03.480 --> 01:34:04.680
 within a certain period of time

01:34:04.680 --> 01:34:05.660
 which is going to be right around

01:34:05.660 --> 01:34:06.900
 the budget time that it completes

01:34:06.900 --> 01:34:08.240
 and we're fighting to get

01:34:08.240 --> 01:34:09.260
 every other project done

01:34:09.260 --> 01:34:10.720
 at the same time.

01:34:10.820 --> 01:34:12.260
 So prioritization has to be part

01:34:12.260 --> 01:34:12.840
 of that as well.

01:34:14.160 --> 01:34:15.700
 Yeah, I'll share a couple thoughts

01:34:15.700 --> 01:34:16.260
 with you, Zach.

01:34:16.400 --> 01:34:17.820
 I mean, it really comes down

01:34:17.820 --> 01:34:18.860
 to the funding mechanism

01:34:18.860 --> 01:34:20.300
 and how you choose to use it

01:34:20.300 --> 01:34:21.120
 because impact fees

01:34:21.120 --> 01:34:22.440
 have to be spent in six years.

01:34:23.160 --> 01:34:24.060
 They have to be spent

01:34:24.060 --> 01:34:24.520
 on those things

01:34:24.520 --> 01:34:25.160
 that you're identified

01:34:25.160 --> 01:34:27.820
 in the IFFP and the IFA.

01:34:28.640 --> 01:34:30.620
 I mean, you can't just

01:34:30.620 --> 01:34:32.260
 willy-nilly appropriate those funds.

01:34:32.420 --> 01:34:33.100
 You have to already

01:34:33.100 --> 01:34:34.180
 pre-appropriate them

01:34:34.180 --> 01:34:36.140
 as you're collecting them.

01:34:36.200 --> 01:34:37.080
 So you're collecting them

01:34:37.080 --> 01:34:38.300
 in order to accomplish

01:34:38.300 --> 01:34:39.280
 and pay for those projects

01:34:39.280 --> 01:34:40.380
 in a six-year time frame.

01:34:41.600 --> 01:34:42.960
 I mean, the wrap tax,

01:34:44.060 --> 01:34:45.300
 if we're going to extract that

01:34:45.300 --> 01:34:46.260
 through the sales tax use,

01:34:46.340 --> 01:34:47.500
 which the citizens said

01:34:47.500 --> 01:34:49.020
 they're happy to see us do it,

01:34:49.060 --> 01:34:49.840
 in my opinion,

01:34:50.180 --> 01:34:54.180
 I feel like we need

01:34:54.180 --> 01:34:55.680
 to expend those resources

01:34:55.680 --> 01:34:57.320
 on behalf of the citizens

01:34:57.320 --> 01:34:58.240
 in a timely manner

01:34:58.240 --> 01:35:00.100
 so that we're not dealing

01:35:00.100 --> 01:35:01.980
 with a deflation of that dollar

01:35:01.980 --> 01:35:03.480
 that we've exacted as a tax

01:35:03.480 --> 01:35:06.280
 through a sales tax exaction.

01:35:07.340 --> 01:35:08.180
 And so I think it needs

01:35:08.180 --> 01:35:09.480
 to be put to community use.

01:35:09.620 --> 01:35:10.480
 And so I understand

01:35:10.480 --> 01:35:11.180
 your question about,

01:35:11.280 --> 01:35:12.320
 well, should we just invest it

01:35:12.320 --> 01:35:13.100
 and we're perpetually

01:35:13.100 --> 01:35:14.240
 pulling interest off of that?

01:35:14.300 --> 01:35:15.180
 There may be a case

01:35:15.180 --> 01:35:16.520
 where that could be made,

01:35:17.700 --> 01:35:18.700
 but that's a pretty hard thing

01:35:18.700 --> 01:35:19.640
 for me because I would just

01:35:19.640 --> 01:35:20.160
 as soon leave it

01:35:20.160 --> 01:35:21.120
 in the citizens' pocket

01:35:21.120 --> 01:35:23.220
 as to take it

01:35:23.220 --> 01:35:24.440
 and invest it on their behalf.

01:35:24.560 --> 01:35:26.260
 I think there's things

01:35:26.260 --> 01:35:27.400
 that government should be doing

01:35:27.400 --> 01:35:27.760
 and there's things

01:35:27.760 --> 01:35:28.120
 that government

01:35:28.120 --> 01:35:28.840
 should not be doing.

01:35:31.740 --> 01:35:32.640
 That's good feedback.

01:35:32.640 --> 01:35:33.940
 The other thing,

01:35:35.280 --> 01:35:35.500
 you know,

01:35:35.600 --> 01:35:37.180
 if you take those funds,

01:35:37.300 --> 01:35:38.140
 wrap tax dollars,

01:35:38.660 --> 01:35:39.880
 and you fund salaries

01:35:39.880 --> 01:35:41.020
 with that,

01:35:41.300 --> 01:35:43.020
 you now create

01:35:43.020 --> 01:35:43.640
 a perpetual need

01:35:44.380 --> 01:35:45.420
 for that resource,

01:35:45.620 --> 01:35:47.340
 which it will expire

01:35:47.340 --> 01:35:48.060
 in 10 years.

01:35:48.620 --> 01:35:49.980
 I'm more just trying

01:35:49.980 --> 01:35:50.740
 to get the councils

01:35:50.740 --> 01:35:52.440
 where everybody's at.

01:35:52.500 --> 01:35:53.020
 So as we're meeting

01:35:53.020 --> 01:35:53.560
 with them

01:35:53.560 --> 01:35:54.740
 and laying it out,

01:35:54.860 --> 01:35:55.740
 so Council Member Wright,

01:35:55.800 --> 01:35:56.660
 what I just heard you say

01:35:56.660 --> 01:35:59.060
 is let's spend it.

01:35:59.140 --> 01:36:00.160
 Let's put it to use

01:36:00.160 --> 01:36:01.480
 in some kind of,

01:36:02.360 --> 01:36:03.640
 wherever we're deficient,

01:36:03.840 --> 01:36:04.100
 right?

01:36:05.420 --> 01:36:06.500
 I think we should put it

01:36:06.500 --> 01:36:07.280
 to public use.

01:36:07.660 --> 01:36:08.420
 If the public

01:36:08.420 --> 01:36:09.440
 is going to contribute it,

01:36:09.680 --> 01:36:10.220
 we ought to put it

01:36:10.220 --> 01:36:10.980
 to public use

01:36:10.980 --> 01:36:11.820
 in a timely manner.

01:36:12.660 --> 01:36:13.000
 I mean,

01:36:13.040 --> 01:36:14.180
 we're looking at funds

01:36:14.180 --> 01:36:15.160
 that we've been sitting on.

01:36:15.380 --> 01:36:16.480
 I don't know how long ago

01:36:16.480 --> 01:36:17.620
 the utility sales happened,

01:36:17.720 --> 01:36:18.580
 but we still have money

01:36:18.580 --> 01:36:19.140
 in accounts

01:36:19.140 --> 01:36:20.660
 that hasn't been spent

01:36:20.660 --> 01:36:22.080
 that is worth half

01:36:22.080 --> 01:36:22.600
 what it was

01:36:22.600 --> 01:36:23.160
 when it was put

01:36:23.160 --> 01:36:23.780
 in those accounts.

01:36:23.920 --> 01:36:24.060
 Now,

01:36:24.120 --> 01:36:25.300
 it's drawn some interest,

01:36:25.980 --> 01:36:27.080
 so it's earned something,

01:36:27.200 --> 01:36:27.920
 but it certainly

01:36:27.920 --> 01:36:29.560
 has been more devalued

01:36:29.560 --> 01:36:30.440
 in my estimation

01:36:30.440 --> 01:36:31.920
 than if we had

01:36:31.920 --> 01:36:33.280
 taken that money,

01:36:33.680 --> 01:36:34.380
 someone had taken

01:36:34.380 --> 01:36:34.700
 that money

01:36:34.700 --> 01:36:35.620
 and appropriated it

01:36:35.620 --> 01:36:36.120
 to something

01:36:36.120 --> 01:36:37.220
 that benefited the public.

01:36:38.320 --> 01:36:39.900
 I think one question

01:36:39.900 --> 01:36:40.960
 that I would ask

01:36:40.960 --> 01:36:42.080
 Council to weigh in on

01:36:42.080 --> 01:36:44.260
 is do we want

01:36:44.260 --> 01:36:44.880
 to prioritize

01:36:44.880 --> 01:36:46.800
 bringing our amenities

01:36:46.800 --> 01:36:47.680
 that we have

